{"topics":[],"grammarPoints":[],"lessons":[],"exercises":[{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The correct collocation for school assignments is 'do homework'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I need to ___ my homework before I can play outside.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To prepare food, the correct verb is 'make'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My mom likes to ___ a big breakfast on Sundays for everyone.","options":["do","make","take","get"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"For being located within a larger area like a city center, 'in' is the correct preposition.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new art gallery is ___ the city center, near the park.","options":["on","at","in","to"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The correct verb used with most ball sports like tennis is 'play'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Do you want to ___ tennis with me this afternoon at the club?","options":["make","do","play","go"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To express possession of an item, the verb 'have' is used.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I ___ a new laptop; it's very fast and light.","options":["do","make","have","take"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"When something is resting on a surface, the preposition 'on' is used.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The cat is sleeping ___ the grass in the garden, enjoying the sun.","options":["in","at","on","by"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To indicate movement towards a destination, the phrase 'go to' is used.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We will ___ the museum next Saturday to see the new exhibition.","options":["go to","go in","go at","go on"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"We use 'do' with 'homework' to mean completing a task.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Students often ___ homework after school.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"We use 'by' to indicate the method of transport.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I go to work ___ bus every day.","options":["on","by","with","in"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"We use 'have' to describe possessing or experiencing a relationship.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister and I ___ a good relationship.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The common collocation for resting is 'take a break'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important to ___ a break when you feel tired.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.12","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To earn money, we use the phrase 'make money'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I need to ___ money to buy food.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.13","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"We use 'speak' to refer to the ability to use a language.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Can you ___ English?","options":["speak","say","tell","talk"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.collocation.14","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"We use 'make' with 'a plan' to mean creating it.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city wants to ___ a new plan for traffic.","options":["make","do","have","take"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Is\" is incorrect for the plural subject \"They\"; the correct form of \"to be\" is \"are.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"They is students at the university.","options":["They","is","students","at the university."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"For the third person singular subject \"He,\" the verb \"like\" needs an \"-s\" ending to be \"likes.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"He like to cook Italian food.","options":["He","like","to cook","Italian food."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"There are\" is incorrect for a singular noun \"a big park\"; the correct phrase is \"There is.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"There are a big park near the city center.","options":["There are","a big park","near the","city center."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The word \"tree\" should be in its plural form, \"trees,\" to match the quantifier \"Many.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Many tree grow in this beautiful forest.","options":["Many tree","grow in","this beautiful","forest."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The indefinite article \"a\" should be \"an\" before a word starting with a vowel sound like \"old.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I bought a new computer and a old mouse.","options":["I bought","a new","computer and","a old mouse."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"How\" is incorrect when asking about a specific item; \"What\" should be used to ask about a favorite sport.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"How is your favorite sport to play on weekends?","options":["How is","your favorite","sport to","play on weekends?"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The verb 'be' must agree with the singular subject 'She', so 'is' should be used instead of 'are'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister is a doctor. She are very busy.","options":["My sister is","a doctor.","She are","very busy."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"For third person singular subjects like 'He', the present simple verb needs an '-s' ending, so 'studies' is correct.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Students usually study in the library. He study there too.","options":["Students usually","study in the library.","He study","there too."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"When referring to a singular noun like 'a big river', the correct phrase is 'There is' not 'There are'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"There are many parks in this city. There are also a big river.","options":["There are many","parks in this city.","There are also","a big river."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The indefinite article 'an' should be used before words that start with a vowel sound, such as 'apple'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I need a ticket for the train. I want to buy a apple.","options":["I need a ticket","for the train.","I want to buy","a apple."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"After 'many', a plural noun is required, so 'projects' should be used instead of 'project'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company has many new project. They are all important.","options":["The company has","many new project.","They are","all important."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.error.12","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"To ask about a thing or concept like 'favorite color', the question word 'What' is used, not 'Who'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"What is your name? Who is your favorite color? It is blue.","options":["What is your name?","Who is your","favorite color?","It is blue."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The present simple verb 'eat' is used to describe a regular habit or routine, which fits the context of 'usually'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"We usually ___ dinner at seven o'clock.","answers":[["eat","have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The phrase 'There is' is used to introduce the existence of a singular noun, 'a big park'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ a big park near my apartment building.","answers":[["There is"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The indefinite article 'a' is used before singular countable nouns starting with a consonant sound, like 'new phone'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"I need ___ new phone; my old one is broken.","answers":[["a"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The plural noun 'trees' is required to agree with the quantifier 'many' and the plural verb 'are'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"There are many beautiful ___ in the forest.","answers":[["trees"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The question word 'What' is used to ask for information about a thing, in this case, a favorite sport.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"\"___ is your favorite sport?\" \"It's tennis.\"","answers":[["What"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The verb 'is' correctly completes the sentence, agreeing with the singular subject 'My sister' in the present tense.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"My sister ___ a doctor.","answers":[["is"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The base form 'study' is used with the pronoun 'We' to express a regular, habitual action in the present simple tense.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"We ___ English every day.","answers":[["study"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'There is' is used to indicate the existence of a singular noun, 'a park', in a specific location.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ a park near my house.","answers":[["There is"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The indefinite article 'a' is used before singular countable nouns that begin with a consonant sound, like 'ticket'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"I need ___ ticket for the bus.","answers":[["a"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.gap.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The plural form 'employees' is required after 'many new' to refer to multiple people working for the company.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The company has many new ___ .","answers":[["employees"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Am is the correct form of the verb to be used with the subject pronoun I in the present simple tense.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Hello, I ___ a new employee here.","options":["am","is","are","be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"There is is used to introduce a singular noun, a big park, indicating its existence or location.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ a big park near my apartment.","options":["There is","There are","Is there","Are there"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The correct plural form of the noun tomato is tomatoes, following the rule for nouns ending in -o.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I need three ___ for this recipe.","options":["tomato","tomatoes","tomatos","a tomato"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Can is the correct modal verb to express ability, and it is followed by the base form of the main verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My brother ___ run very fast. He's a great athlete.","options":["can","cans","is can","can to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"On is the correct preposition to describe something resting directly on a surface, like a bird on a branch.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The small bird is sitting ___ the branch.","options":["on","in","under","next"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"What is the appropriate question word to ask for information about an object or its characteristics.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ is your new phone? It looks very modern.","options":["What","Who","Where","How"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"For a plural subject like scientists in the present simple, the base form of the verb do is used.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Scientists often ___ experiments in the lab.","options":["do","does","doing","to do"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Its is the possessive adjective for it, showing that the colors belong to the painting.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"This is my favorite painting. ___ colors are so vibrant.","options":["Its","It's","Their","His"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"For a singular third-person subject like 'my sister', the correct form of the verb 'be' in the present simple is 'is'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister ___ a doctor.","options":["am","are","is","be"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"For a plural subject like 'students', the base form of the verb 'study' is used in the present simple.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Students usually ___ in the library.","options":["studying","studies","study","to study"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The present continuous 'are going' is used to describe an action happening at the moment of speaking, indicated by 'right now'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"They ___ to the airport right now.","options":["go","goes","are going","going"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.13","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"To indicate the existence of a singular item, 'a new bank', the correct phrase is 'There is'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ a new bank on this street.","options":["There are","There is","Is there","Are there"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.14","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The indefinite article 'an' is used before words that start with a vowel sound, like 'interesting'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I read ___ interesting article today.","options":["a","an","the","some"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.15","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The correct irregular plural form of 'person' is 'people', which is used with quantifiers like 'many'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Many ___ feel better after talking.","options":["person","persons","people","peoples"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.grammar.16","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"To show possession for a female subject 'she', the correct possessive adjective is 'her'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She speaks French, but ___ native language is Spanish.","options":["my","your","his","her"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The correct order for a simple descriptive sentence is subject, verb 'is', then the adjective.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","building","is","tall."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The sentence structure requires the subject 'They' followed by the verb 'work' and then the prepositional phrase 'in an office'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["They","work","in","an","office."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The phrase 'There are' introduces the existence of plural nouns, followed by the quantity and the noun.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["There","are","many","trees."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The sentence correctly places the subject 'The apple', followed by 'is', and then the prepositional phrase 'on the table'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","apple","is","on","the","table."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"For a 'where' question with 'is', the structure is 'Where' + 'is' + the subject 'the gym'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Where","is","the","gym?"]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The possessive adjective 'Her' comes before the noun 'painting', followed by 'is' and the adjective 'beautiful'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Her","painting","is","beautiful."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The correct word order for a simple statement with 'be' is subject, verb, then the complement.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","is","my","sister."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Time expressions like 'every day' usually come at the end of a sentence in present simple.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["They","study","English","every","day."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The phrase 'There are' introduces plural nouns, followed by any modifiers.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["There","are","many","parks."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The indefinite article 'a' comes before singular countable nouns starting with a consonant sound.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["I","have","a","ticket."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Prepositions like 'on' indicate location and come before the place name in a sentence.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","office","is","on","Main","Street."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.order.12","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Question words like 'What' begin a question, followed by the verb 'is' and the subject.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["What","is","your","name?"]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that Mr. Chen is an accountant.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Mr. Chen works at a large office downtown. He is an accountant and helps companies with their money.","question":"What is Mr. Chen's job?","options":["A doctor","An accountant","A manager","A driver"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage mentions that the new library has a glass roof.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city of Portville has many tall buildings. The new library is very modern and has a glass roof.","question":"What kind of roof does the new library have?","options":["A metal roof","A tile roof","A glass roof","A wooden roof"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that Elena's favorite recipe is chocolate chip cookies.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Elena loves to bake on weekends. Her favorite recipe is chocolate chip cookies, which she makes for her family.","question":"What does Elena like to bake?","options":["Bread","Cakes","Cookies","Pies"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage says Mark lifts weights and runs on the treadmill.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Mark goes to the gym every morning. He lifts weights and runs on the treadmill for an hour.","question":"What does Mark do at the gym?","options":["Swims","Lifts weights","Plays tennis","Rides a bike"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the smartphone has a good camera for taking pictures and videos.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"My new smartphone has a very good camera. I can take clear pictures and videos with it.","question":"What can I do with my new smartphone?","options":["Drive a car","Cook dinner","Take pictures","Build a house"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage mentions that fish swim in the river.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The park has many old trees and a small river. Birds sing in the trees, and fish swim in the water.","question":"What is in the park's river?","options":["Boats","Fish","Rocks","Flowers"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that Sarah enjoys painting landscapes.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Sarah enjoys painting landscapes. She uses bright colors to show the mountains and the sky in her artwork.","question":"What does Sarah like to paint?","options":["Portraits","Animals","Landscapes","Buildings"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage clearly states that David studies English.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"David studies English every day. He wants to speak well.","question":"What does David study?","options":["Math","History","English","Science"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage explicitly states that the train leaves at 9 AM.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The train leaves at 9 AM. Please be at the station early.","question":"When does the train leave?","options":["8 AM","9 AM","10 AM","11 AM"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states, \"Anna feels happy today.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Anna feels happy today. She likes sunny weather.","question":"How does Anna feel?","options":["Sad","Tired","Happy","Angry"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.12","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage says that French is a common language in Canada.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Many people in Canada speak French. It is a common language there.","question":"What language is common in Canada?","options":["Spanish","German","French","Italian"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.13","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that Tom reads the newspaper every morning.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Tom reads the newspaper every morning. He likes to know the news.","question":"What does Tom read?","options":["A book","A magazine","A newspaper","A letter"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.reading.14","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage clearly states that the city built a new park.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city built a new park. It has many trees and flowers.","question":"What did the city build?","options":["A school","A park","A road","A bridge"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.01","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The verb 'to be' changes from 'am' to 'is' when the subject changes from 'I' to 'She'.","kind":"transformation","source":"I am a doctor.","instruction":"Begin with 'She'.","answers":["She is a doctor."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.02","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"For the present simple tense, a singular third-person subject like 'A bird' requires the verb to end in '-s'.","kind":"transformation","source":"Birds fly.","instruction":"Begin with 'A bird'.","answers":["A bird flies."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.03","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"To state the existence of multiple items, 'There are' is used, followed by the plural noun.","kind":"transformation","source":"Five parks are in the city.","instruction":"Use the word 'are'.","answers":["There are five parks in the city."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.04","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The indefinite article 'an' is used before words starting with a vowel sound, and 'a' before consonant sounds.","kind":"transformation","source":"I want apple and banana.","instruction":"Use the word 'an'.","answers":["I want an apple and a banana."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.05","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The preposition 'under' indicates a position directly below something, replacing 'on' for a different location.","kind":"transformation","source":"The phone is on the table.","instruction":"Use the word 'under'.","answers":["The phone is under the table."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.06","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The question word 'What' is used to ask for information about a thing or activity.","kind":"transformation","source":"My favorite sport is swimming.","instruction":"Begin with 'What'.","answers":["What is your favorite sport?"]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.07","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The instruction requires transforming the sentence to use the subject pronoun 'She' with the correct form of the verb 'be'.","kind":"transformation","source":"My sister is a doctor.","instruction":"Begin with 'She'.","answers":["She is a doctor."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.08","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"To make a present simple sentence negative, 'do not' or 'don't' is placed before the main verb.","kind":"transformation","source":"I study English every day.","instruction":"Use the word 'not'.","answers":["I do not study English every day.","I don't study English every day."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.09","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The phrase 'There is' is used to state the existence or presence of a singular noun.","kind":"transformation","source":"A park is in the city center.","instruction":"Begin with 'There'.","answers":["There is a park in the city center."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.10","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"When referring to more than one item, the noun must be changed to its plural form, in this case, 'bus' becomes 'buses'.","kind":"transformation","source":"I see one bus.","instruction":"Use the word 'two'.","answers":["I see two buses."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.11","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The possessive adjective 'his' replaces 'the company of my father' to show ownership concisely.","kind":"transformation","source":"This is the company of my father.","instruction":"Use the word 'his'.","answers":["This is his company."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a1.transform.12","level":"A1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"To ask about the specific time of an event, the question word phrase 'What time' is used.","kind":"transformation","source":"The meeting is at 3 PM.","instruction":"Begin with 'What'.","answers":["What time is the meeting?"]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The correct collocation for arranging an appointment or discussion is 'set a meeting'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I need to ___ a meeting with my team to discuss the new project.","options":["make","do","set","take"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.02","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To prepare a table with plates and cutlery for a meal, the correct phrase is 'lay the table'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Could you please ___ the table before dinner?","options":["put","lay","make","place"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"When a place provides a particular sight, the common collocation is 'offer a view'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city center has many tall buildings that ___ a great view.","options":["give","make","offer","show"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The standard verb used with 'exercise' to mean performing physical activity is 'do'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important to ___ exercise every day to stay healthy.","options":["make","do","take","play"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To restore power to an electronic device's battery, the correct verb is 'charge'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My phone battery is very low; I need to ___ it soon.","options":["charge","fill","power","load"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To reduce the amount of energy used, the correct verb is 'save'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We should try to ___ energy by turning off lights when we leave a room.","options":["save","keep","hold","store"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"When a musical group performs for an audience, the common collocation is 'give a concert'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The band will ___ a concert next month at the new stadium.","options":["make","give","play","hold"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Take the bus\" is the standard collocation for using public transportation.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We usually ___ the bus to work because it's cheaper than driving.","options":["take","ride","drive","go"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Pay attention\" is the correct and common collocation for focusing on something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Students should always ___ attention in class to understand the lesson.","options":["give","make","pay","do"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Spend money\" is the correct collocation for using money to buy things or services.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important to ___ money carefully so you don't run out.","options":["spend","use","give","make"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Take care of\" is the correct phrasal verb meaning to look after someone.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister always ___ care of her younger brother when our parents are out.","options":["makes","takes","does","gives"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Have a rest\" is the standard collocation for taking a period of relaxation.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"After working all day, I like to ___ a rest and relax.","options":["make","do","have","get"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.13","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Make mistakes\" is the correct and common collocation for doing something incorrectly.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"When you learn a new language, it's normal to ___ mistakes.","options":["do","make","have","get"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.collocation.14","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Write an article\" is the correct collocation for producing a piece of writing for publication.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The local newspaper will ___ an article about the new park next week.","options":["write","make","do","tell"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The verb \"finish\" needs to be in the past simple form \"finished\" to match the past time reference \"late last night.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She finish her big project late last night, and then went home.","options":["She finish","her big project","late last night,","and then went home."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The correct structure for future plans with \"going to\" requires \"to\" after \"going\" and before the base verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My old phone is broken, so I am going buy a new one tomorrow.","options":["My old phone","is broken, so I","am going buy","a new one tomorrow."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"For short adjectives like \"tall,\" the comparative form is created by adding \"-er,\" not by using \"more.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new skyscraper is more tall than the old city hall, which was built in 1920.","options":["The new skyscraper","is more tall","than the old city hall,","which was built in 1920."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Sugar\" is an uncountable noun, so \"much\" should be used with it, not \"many.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We don't have many sugar left for the cake, so I need to buy some.","options":["We don't have","many sugar left","for the cake,","so I need to buy some."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The modal verb \"should\" is followed directly by the base form of the verb, without \"to.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If you want to feel better, you should to exercise at least three times a week.","options":["If you want","to feel better,","you should to exercise","at least three times a week."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The past simple form of 'visit' is 'visited' for an action completed in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Last summer, we visit a small town near the coast. We really enjoyed the quiet atmosphere.","options":["Last summer, we visit a small town","near the coast.","We really enjoyed","the quiet atmosphere."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"To talk about an experience that has not happened up to now, the present perfect tense is required.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She never study abroad, but she hopes to do it next year.","options":["She never","study abroad,","but she hopes","to do it next year."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"'People' is a countable noun, so 'many' should be used instead of 'much' in negative sentences.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"There isn't much people at the conference, so it's easy to find a seat.","options":["There isn't","much people","at the conference,","so it's easy to find a seat."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The modal verb 'should' is followed directly by the base form of the main verb, without 'to'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If you feel stressed, you should to take a break and relax for a while.","options":["If you feel","stressed, you should","to take a break","and relax for a while."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.error.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The correct preposition for 'weekends' is 'on' when referring to a recurring time.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister usually calls me in the weekends to chat about our week.","options":["My sister usually","calls me","in the weekends","to chat about our week."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The past simple form 'started' is used to describe a completed action in the past, indicated by 'Last year'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"Last year, Sarah ___ a new project at her company, and it was very successful.","answers":[["started"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The structure 'be going to' is used to express future plans or intentions, indicated by 'next year'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"They ___ to build a new park in the city center next year.","answers":[["are going"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The comparative adjective 'larger' is used to compare the size of the Amazon rainforest to other forests.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The Amazon rainforest is much ___ than any other forest in the world.","answers":[["larger"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Should' is used here to give advice or make a recommendation for improving stamina.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If you want to improve your stamina, you ___ run every day.","answers":[["should"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The preposition 'on' is used for specific days of the week or parts of a day when a specific day is mentioned.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The new software update will be available ___ Tuesday morning.","answers":[["on"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The past simple form 'rented' is used for a completed action in the past.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"Last summer, we ___ a small apartment near the beach for our vacation.","answers":[["rented"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The structure 'is going to' expresses a future plan or intention.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The city council ___ to build a new park next year.","answers":[["is going","plans"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The comparative adjective 'faster' is used to compare two items.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"This new laptop is much ___ than my old one.","answers":[["faster"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Should' is used to give advice or make a recommendation.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If you're feeling stressed, you ___ try meditating for a few minutes each day.","answers":[["should"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.gap.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'At' is the correct preposition to use with specific times.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The news report will be broadcast ___ 7 o'clock this evening.","answers":[["at"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Started is the correct past simple form of the regular verb start needed to describe a completed action in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Last year, Maria ___ a new project at her company.","options":["start","starting","started","starts"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.02","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Fell is the correct past simple form of the irregular verb fall to describe a completed action in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The old building ___ down in 1998 to make space for a park.","options":["fell","fall","fallen","falling"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The present perfect uses have/has + past participle to talk about experiences, so run is the correct form.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Have you ever ___ a marathon? It's a very challenging race.","options":["run","ran","running","runs"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Is going to is used to express a future plan or prediction based on present evidence.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I think the new software ___ make our work much easier next month.","options":["is going to","go to","going to","will going to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Bigger is the correct comparative form of big used to compare two things.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The Amazon rainforest is much ___ than any other forest in the world.","options":["big","bigger","biggest","more big"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Never correctly indicates that he does not eat vegetables, contrasting with his preference for fruit.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My brother ___ eats vegetables; he prefers fruit.","options":["never","always","often","usually"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Research is an uncountable noun, so it does not take an 's' or an indefinite article.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We need to do more ___ before we can publish our findings.","options":["research","reasearches","a research","some researches"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Should is used to give advice or make a recommendation.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If you want to improve your drawing skills, you ___ practice every day.","options":["should","must","have to","can"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The past simple form of \"study\" is \"studied,\" used here to describe a completed action in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Last year, I ___ a new language course online.","options":["study","studied","studying","studies"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Went\" is the irregular past simple form of \"go,\" correctly used for a completed action in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We ___ to the mountains for our vacation last summer.","options":["go","going","went","gone"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Seen\" is the past participle of \"see,\" required to form the present perfect for an experience.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Have you ever ___ a live concert?","options":["see","saw","seen","seeing"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Is going to\" is the correct structure for expressing a future plan or intention.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city ___ build a new park next year.","options":["is going to","go to","going to","will going to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.13","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"For longer adjectives like \"expensive,\" the comparative form is created using \"more.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"This new laptop is ___ than my old one.","options":["expensiver","more expensive","most expensive","much expensive"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.14","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Often\" is the correct adverb of frequency to describe how frequently an action occurs.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister ___ visits us on Sundays.","options":["often","oftenly","more often","very often"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.15","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Much\" is used with uncountable nouns like \"time\" in negative sentences.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I don't have ___ time to relax these days.","options":["many","a lot","much","some"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.grammar.16","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"At\" is used to specify a precise time, such as \"7 PM.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The online language class starts ___ 7 PM every Tuesday.","options":["in","on","at","for"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The subject comes first, followed by the past simple verb, then the adverb and time expression.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","worked","hard","yesterday."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.02","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The present perfect is formed with 'has' for third person singular subjects, followed by the past participle.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","has","studied","this","topic."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The 'going to' future is formed with 'be' + 'going to' + base verb, following the subject.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["They","are","going","to","design","a","building."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Comparative adjectives like 'sweeter' are followed by 'than' when comparing two things.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["This","cake","is","sweeter","than","that","one."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Adverbs of frequency like 'often' usually come before the main verb in a sentence.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["We","often","go","to","the","gym."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"'Should' is a modal verb that comes before the base form of the main verb to give advice.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["You","should","update","your","software."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The subject comes before the regular past simple verb, followed by the prepositional phrase and time expression.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["They","traveled","to","Paris","last","year."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The structure \"subject + be + going to + base verb\" is used to express future plans and intentions.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","city","is","going","to","build","a","new","park."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"More\" precedes longer adjectives in comparative structures, followed by \"than\" for comparison.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["This","book","is","more","interesting","than","that","one."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Adverbs of frequency like \"often\" typically come before the main verb in a sentence.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["We","often","visit","our","grandparents."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"Should\" is a modal verb followed by the base form of the main verb to give advice.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["You","should","relax","more","often."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.order.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"In the present perfect, \"never\" is placed between the auxiliary verb \"has/have\" and the past participle.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","has","never","seen","that","movie."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The message clearly states the team meeting is \"on Friday at 10:00 AM.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Reminder: The team meeting is on Friday at 10:00 AM in the main office. Please prepare your updates. Don't be late!","question":"When is the team meeting?","options":["On Thursday","At 10:00 AM","In the main office","On Friday at 10:00 AM"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.02","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage explicitly states, \"I will cook pasta with vegetables.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"For dinner tonight, we need milk, eggs, and bread from the store. I will cook pasta with vegetables. Don't forget the list!","question":"What will be cooked for dinner?","options":["Milk and eggs","Pasta with vegetables","Bread and pasta","A list of items"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The message clearly states the new library \"opens next Monday.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new library opens next Monday at 9 AM. It has three floors and a cafe. Come visit us for books and coffee!","question":"When does the new library open?","options":["Today","Next Monday","At 9 AM","On three floors"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The message clearly states the gym is \"closed for cleaning this Saturday.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Gym is closed for cleaning this Saturday, July 15th. It will reopen Sunday morning. Sorry for any inconvenience.","question":"When is the gym closed?","options":["On Sunday","For cleaning","This Saturday","Sunday morning"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the laptop will arrive \"between 2 PM and 4 PM tomorrow,\" which is tomorrow afternoon.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Your new laptop will arrive between 2 PM and 4 PM tomorrow. Please be home to sign for the delivery. Thank you.","question":"When will the laptop arrive?","options":["Today","Tomorrow afternoon","At 2 PM","To sign for it"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage clearly states the park trails are wet \"after the rain.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Park trails are wet after the rain. Please wear waterproof shoes if you plan to hike. Enjoy the fresh air!","question":"Why are the park trails wet?","options":["For hiking","After the rain","To wear shoes","For fresh air"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.07","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the exhibition is \"at the City Gallery.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The art exhibition 'Colors of the City' starts on October 5th at the City Gallery. It features local artists. Free entry!","question":"Where is the art exhibition?","options":["On October 5th","Free entry","At the City Gallery","Local artists"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage clearly states, \"Please send the invoice to Mr. Chen.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Please send the invoice to Mr. Chen by Friday. He needs it to approve the payment for the new office supplies.","question":"What should be sent to Mr. Chen?","options":["Office supplies","An invoice","A payment","An approval"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The essay is due \"next Monday\" and must be submitted \"before 5 PM\" on that day.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Remember, your essay on local history is due next Monday. Please submit it online before 5 PM. No late submissions will be accepted.","question":"When must the essay be submitted?","options":["By 5 PM today","Before next Monday","By next Monday at 5 PM","After next Monday"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The announcement states the train \"will depart from Platform 4 today, not Platform 2.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Attention passengers: The 9:30 AM train to Weston will depart from Platform 4 today, not Platform 2. We apologize for the change.","question":"What is different about the train to Weston?","options":["Its departure time","Its destination","Its platform number","Its arrival time"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage advises, \"Try taking a short walk outside\" to help with stress.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Feeling stressed? Try taking a short walk outside. Even 15 minutes of fresh air can help clear your mind and improve your mood.","question":"What is suggested to reduce stress?","options":["Staying indoors","Working longer","Taking a short walk","Thinking more"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states, \"She wants to practice her Spanish for an upcoming trip.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Maria is looking for a language exchange partner. She wants to practice her Spanish for an upcoming trip. She can help with English.","question":"What language does Maria want to practice?","options":["English","Spanish","French","German"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.13","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The news update is about \"plans for a new community park downtown\" being approved.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Local news update: The city council approved plans for a new community park downtown. Construction is expected to begin next spring.","question":"What is the main news topic?","options":["A new city council","Downtown construction","A new community park","Spring weather"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.reading.14","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The notice clearly states the playground is closed \"for safety repairs.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Notice: The Central Park playground will be closed all week for safety repairs. We expect it to reopen next Monday. Thank you.","question":"Why is the playground closed?","options":["For a holiday","For safety repairs","For a new design","For a party"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.01","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The word 'complete' is a synonym for 'finish' and fits the past simple negative structure.","kind":"transformation","source":"They did not finish the report on Friday.","instruction":"Use the word 'complete'.","answers":["They did not complete the report on Friday."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.02","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The phrase 'are going to' expresses a future plan, replacing 'plan to' while keeping the meaning.","kind":"transformation","source":"We plan to visit the new art gallery next month.","instruction":"Use the word 'going'.","answers":["We are going to visit the new art gallery next month."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.03","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The comparative form 'taller than' correctly expresses the height difference between the two bridges.","kind":"transformation","source":"The old bridge is not as tall as the new one.","instruction":"Use the word 'taller'.","answers":["The new bridge is taller than the old one.","The new one is taller than the old bridge."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.04","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The phrase 'have to' correctly expresses necessity or obligation, replacing 'it is necessary for'.","kind":"transformation","source":"It is necessary for all employees to wear safety gear.","instruction":"Use the word 'have'.","answers":["All employees have to wear safety gear."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.05","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The preposition 'on' is used to indicate a specific day or part of a day when an event occurs.","kind":"transformation","source":"The workshop is scheduled for Tuesday afternoon.","instruction":"Use the word 'on'.","answers":["The workshop is on Tuesday afternoon."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.06","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The verb 'love' can replace 'really enjoy' to express strong preference, followed by the -ing form.","kind":"transformation","source":"I really enjoy trying new recipes.","instruction":"Use the word 'love'.","answers":["I love trying new recipes."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.08","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The sentence is transformed from active to passive voice, using the past participle of the irregular verb 'buy'.","kind":"transformation","source":"She bought a new car last month.","instruction":"Begin with 'A new car...'","answers":["A new car was bought by her last month."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.09","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The sentence is transformed to express a lack of past experience using the present perfect tense with 'never'.","kind":"transformation","source":"This is my first time studying abroad.","instruction":"Use the word 'never'.","answers":["I have never studied abroad before."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.10","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The sentence is rewritten to express a future plan using the 'be going to' structure.","kind":"transformation","source":"My plan is to start exercising next week.","instruction":"Use the word 'going'.","answers":["I am going to start exercising next week."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.11","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The sentence is transformed to express a comparison of inequality using 'not as...as'.","kind":"transformation","source":"My sister is taller than me.","instruction":"Begin with 'I am not...'","answers":["I am not as tall as my sister."]},{"id":"ex.placement.a2.transform.12","level":"A2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The sentence is rewritten to give advice using the modal verb 'should'.","kind":"transformation","source":"It's a good idea to use public transport in the city center.","instruction":"Use the word 'should'.","answers":["You should use public transport in the city center."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"“Make a profit” is the standard collocation used to describe gaining money from a business.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company hopes to ___ a profit by the end of the fiscal year.","options":["earn","make","get","take"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"“Miss a flight” is the correct collocation used when one fails to board an airplane on time.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We had to rush to the airport so we wouldn't ___ our flight.","options":["lose","miss","fail","drop"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The adjective “worried” is typically followed by the preposition “about” when expressing concern for something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She was very worried ___ her upcoming job interview.","options":["for","about","with","on"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"“Fluent in” is the correct collocation to describe someone's ability to speak a language easily and well.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"To work as a translator, you need to be ___ in at least two languages.","options":["fluent","skilled","expert","good"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.13","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"The verb “report” is commonly followed by “on” when a journalist is covering a specific topic.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The journalist was asked to ___ on the recent economic changes.","options":["report","tell","speak","inform"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.14","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"“Spend time” is the correct collocation used to describe dedicating a period to an activity or person.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important to ___ quality time with your family.","options":["pass","spend","give","take"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.15","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Make a profit\" is the standard collocation used to describe generating financial gain for a business.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Many small businesses struggle to ____ a profit in their first year.","options":["make","do","get","earn"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.16","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Pay attention\" is the correct and idiomatic phrase meaning to focus or concentrate on something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important for students to ____ attention in class if they want to understand the lesson.","options":["give","pay","put","take"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.17","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Make sure\" is the common collocation meaning to confirm or verify something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Before we left for our trip, we had to ____ sure we had all our documents.","options":["make","do","get","take"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.18","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Make time\" is the idiomatic expression for scheduling or creating an opportunity for something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"To improve your mental wellbeing, it's helpful to ____ time for relaxation each day.","options":["make","take","find","get"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.19","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Take an opportunity\" is the correct and natural collocation meaning to seize a chance.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"To improve your language skills, you should ____ every opportunity to speak.","options":["take","get","make","use"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.20","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Broadcast\" is the specific verb used for transmitting news or programs via television or radio.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The local news channel decided to ____ a live report from the city center.","options":["make","do","give","broadcast"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.collocation.21","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Keep in touch\" is the common idiom meaning to maintain communication with someone.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It's important to ____ in touch with family members who live far away.","options":["keep","stay","hold","remain"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Used to\" is the correct form for past habits or states that no longer exist.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My colleague use to work in a different department, but she moved last year.","options":["My colleague use to","work in a different department,","but she moved","last year."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Which\" or \"that\" should be used to refer to things in a defining relative clause, not \"who\".","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new skyscraper, who stands over 300 meters tall, offers incredible views of the city.","options":["The new skyscraper,","who stands over","300 meters tall, offers","incredible views of the city."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The past simple passive form requires the past participle of the main verb, so \"prepared\" is correct.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The special dessert was prepare by the head chef for the banquet last night.","options":["The special dessert","was prepare by","the head chef for","the banquet last night."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.04","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Although\" is a conjunction that introduces a subordinate clause and cannot be used alone at the end of a sentence.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The park is beautiful in autumn, the leaves change color, although.","options":["The park is beautiful","in autumn, the leaves","change color,","although."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.05","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The past simple is used for actions completed at a specific time in the past, indicated by \"in 2022\".","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Scientists have discovered a new planet in 2022, which orbits a distant star.","options":["Scientists have discovered","a new planet in 2022,","which orbits","a distant star."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The verb \"enjoy\" is typically followed by a gerund (-ing form) when referring to an activity.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My brother really enjoys to play basketball with his friends on weekends.","options":["My brother really","enjoys to play","basketball with his","friends on weekends."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"Use the past simple for actions completed at a specific time in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I have visited Paris last year for a conference, and I really enjoyed the architecture there.","options":["I have visited Paris","last year for a conference,","and I really enjoyed","the architecture there."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The past simple passive uses 'was/were' followed by the past participle of the main verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"A new community park was build in the city center by local volunteers just last month.","options":["A new community park","was build in the city center","by local volunteers","just last month."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"Use 'whose' to show possession in a relative clause, referring to the lectures belonging to the professor.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The professor, who lectures are always engaging, is known for her innovative teaching methods.","options":["The professor,","who lectures are always engaging,","is known for her","innovative teaching methods."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"'Don't have to' expresses lack of obligation, while 'mustn't' expresses prohibition.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"You mustn't pay for the online course if you are a registered student at the university.","options":["You mustn't pay","for the online course","if you are a registered","student at the university."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The phrasal verb 'look after' means to take care of someone and does not require the preposition 'on'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My older sister always looked after on me when our parents were away for work.","options":["My older sister","always looked after","on me when our parents","were away for work."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.error.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"'Although' introduces a contrasting clause, so 'but' is not needed to connect the two parts of the sentence.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Although it was raining, but we decided to go for a walk in the park anyway.","options":["Although it was raining,","but we decided to","go for a walk","in the park anyway."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The phrase \"for five years now\" indicates an action that started in the past and continues to the present, requiring the present perfect.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"I ___ for this company for five years now, and I still enjoy the challenges.","answers":[["have worked"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"\"Used to\" describes a past habit or state that no longer happens, contrasting with the current situation.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"Before the new highway was built, people ___ drive through the town center to get to the coast.","answers":[["used to"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The subject \"this traditional bread\" receives the action, and the action happens generally, requiring the present simple passive.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"This traditional bread ___ with a special type of flour from the region, giving it a unique flavor.","answers":[["is made"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.04","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The first conditional describes a real possibility in the future, using the present simple in the \"if\" clause.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If it ___ tomorrow, we will have to cancel the outdoor concert and move it indoors.","answers":[["rains"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"\"Which\" introduces a defining relative clause that provides essential information about the smartphone.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The new smartphone, ___ has a much longer battery life, will be released next month.","answers":[["which"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The past simple is used for completed actions at a specific time in the past, indicated by 'when I was younger'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"I ___ to Paris three times when I was younger, but I haven't been back since.","answers":[["went"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The past simple passive is used because the action happened in the past and the focus is on the report, not who wrote it.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The news report ___ by a team of investigative journalists last night.","answers":[["was written"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Who' is used to introduce a defining relative clause that identifies the person being discussed.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The architect ___ designed this building also worked on the city hall.","answers":[["who"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Mustn't' is used to express a strong prohibition or something that is not allowed.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"Students ___ use their phones during the exam; it's strictly forbidden.","answers":[["mustn't","must not"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The second conditional uses the past simple in the 'if' clause to describe an unreal or hypothetical situation.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If I ___ a million dollars, I would buy a small island.","answers":[["had"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.gap.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Chill out' is a common phrasal verb meaning to relax or calm down.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"After a long day at work, I just want to ___ and relax.","answers":[["chill out","lie down"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Last week' indicates a specific time in the past, requiring the past simple tense.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My colleague ___ a new project last week, and it's already going well.","options":["started","has started","starts","is starting"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'For' is used to indicate a duration of time, such as 'decades'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Scientists have been studying the effects of climate change ___ decades.","options":["since","for","during","while"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The present perfect continuous describes an action that started in the past and continued up to the present, explaining a current state.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I'm exhausted because I ___ all morning at the gym.","options":["exercised","have been exercising","have exercised","was exercising"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.05","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Used to' describes a past habit or state that no longer happens.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"When I was a child, we ___ go camping in the mountains every summer.","options":["used to","would to","use to","are used to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The zero conditional describes general truths or scientific facts, using the present simple in both clauses.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If you ___ water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils.","options":["heated","will heat","heat","are heating"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The first conditional describes a real or possible situation in the future, using present simple in the 'if' clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If the internet connection ___ stable, I'll be able to finish my online presentation.","options":["is","will be","was","would be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The second conditional describes an unreal or hypothetical situation in the present or future, using past simple in the 'if' clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If I ___ more free time, I would definitely take an art class.","options":["have","will have","would have","had"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase \"when I was younger\" indicates a specific completed period in the past, requiring the past simple tense.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"I ___ to Paris three times when I was younger, but I haven't been back since.","options":["went","have gone","go","am going"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Which\" is used to introduce a non-defining relative clause that adds extra information about a thing, in this case, \"the new park.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new park, ___ opened last month, has already become very popular with local families.","options":["who","which","where","what"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The verb \"advise\" is followed by an object and then the to-infinitive form of the verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My professor advised me ___ more research before submitting my final paper.","options":["doing","do","to do","did"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Can't\" is used to express a strong deduction that something is impossible or highly unlikely based on evidence.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She looks exhausted. She ___ have slept well last night.","options":["mustn't","doesn't","can't","isn't"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.13","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"In a first conditional sentence, the 'if' clause uses the present simple tense to describe a possible future condition.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If we ___ our sales targets this quarter, we'll receive a bonus.","options":["will meet","meet","met","would meet"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.14","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The past simple passive \"was written\" is used because the action happened in the past (\"last week\") and the report is the recipient of the action.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The news report ___ by a team of investigative journalists last week.","options":["wrote","is written","was written","writes"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.15","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Used to\" describes a past habit or a state that was true in the past but is no longer true.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"When I was a child, my grandparents ___ live in a house with a big garden.","options":["use to","would to","used to","did use"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.grammar.16","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"However\" is used to introduce a statement that contrasts with something previously said, often following a semicolon or period.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Learning a new language can be challenging; ___, the rewards are immense.","options":["although","however","despite","even"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The present perfect is used with 'for' to describe an action that started in the past and continues to the present.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","has","worked","at","this","company","for","five","years."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The phrase 'used to' describes a past state or habit that no longer exists.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["This","old","building","used","to","be","a","library."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The present simple passive is formed with 'are' + past participle to describe general truths or routine actions.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["New","research","findings","are","often","published","in","journals."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.04","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The first conditional describes a real possibility in the future, using 'if + present simple' and 'will + base verb'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["If","it","rains","tomorrow,","we","will","stay","indoors."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.05","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The verb 'enjoy' is followed by the gerund (-ing form) of another verb.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["He","enjoys","cooking","new","recipes","every","weekend."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"A question tag uses an auxiliary verb and pronoun to ask for confirmation, matching the main clause's tense and polarity.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","new","software","is","very","fast,","isn't","it?"],"alternatives":[["The","new","software","isn't","very","fast,","is","it?"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The present perfect is used for experiences up to the present, often with 'times'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["I","have","visited","Paris","three","times."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The past simple passive is formed with 'was/were' + past participle to describe an action completed in the past.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","new","bridge","was","opened","last","week."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The verb 'enjoy' is typically followed by a gerund (-ing form) when referring to an activity.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","enjoys","learning","new","languages."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The first conditional describes a real possibility in the future, using 'if + present simple' and 'will + base verb'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["If","you","save","money,","you","will","buy","a","house."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"When reporting a past statement, the tense often shifts back, so 'I am tired' becomes 'he was tired'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["He","said","that","he","was","tired."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.order.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"'Must be' is used to express a strong deduction or certainty about a present situation.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","must","be","very","happy."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The email clearly states it is 'a quick reminder' about the sales report deadline.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Hi team, just a quick reminder that the quarterly sales report is due by Friday. Please ensure all your figures are updated and submitted to Maria by then so she can compile the final document.","question":"What is the main purpose of this email?","options":["To ask for a new report","To remind about a deadline","To introduce Maria","To discuss sales figures"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The text highlights the discovery of bacteria and its potential to solve plastic pollution.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Researchers have discovered a new type of bacteria that can break down plastic waste much faster than previously known methods. This breakthrough could offer a sustainable solution to the growing problem of plastic pollution.","question":"What is the main idea of this text?","options":["Plastic waste is a big problem.","Scientists found new bacteria.","New bacteria can help with plastic.","Researchers are studying pollution."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage describes the library's 'unique curved glass facade and a rooftop garden' as key features.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new city library, designed by architect Lena Petrova, features a unique curved glass facade and a rooftop garden. It aims to be a community hub, offering not just books but also co-working spaces and a cafe.","question":"What is special about the new library?","options":["It has many books.","It was designed by Lena Petrova.","It has a curved glass front and a garden.","It is a community hub."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.04","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The text advises preheating the oven to its highest temperature with the pizza stone inside for at least 30 minutes.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"For a perfect crispy crust on your homemade pizza, preheat your oven to its highest temperature with the pizza stone inside for at least 30 minutes. This ensures the stone is very hot when the pizza goes in.","question":"What is the advice for a crispy pizza crust?","options":["Use a pizza stone.","Bake at a high temperature.","Preheat the oven and stone well.","Put the pizza in quickly."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.05","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the exhibition 'explores the relationship between city life and nature'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The exhibition 'Urban Echoes' by artist Kai Chen explores the relationship between city life and nature through a series of mixed-media sculptures. Visitors praised the thought-provoking use of recycled materials.","question":"What is 'Urban Echoes' about?","options":["Kai Chen's life","Recycled materials","City life and nature","Mixed-media sculptures"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The text clearly states, 'If you feel pain, it's a sign to stop and rest, not to push harder'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Regular exercise is crucial for maintaining good health, but it's important to listen to your body. If you feel pain, it's a sign to stop and rest, not to push harder, to prevent injuries.","question":"What should you do if you feel pain during exercise?","options":["Push harder","Stop and rest","Exercise more often","Ignore the pain"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage highlights 'an extended battery life' and 'an improved camera' as the main enhancements.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new smartphone model boasts an extended battery life, lasting up to two full days on a single charge, and an improved camera with advanced low-light capabilities. These features aim to enhance user experience.","question":"What are two main improvements of the new phone?","options":["It is cheaper and faster.","It has a bigger screen and new apps.","It has longer battery life and a better camera.","It is lighter and more colorful."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.08","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage explicitly states that the growth is \"mainly due to our new marketing campaign.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Our company's sales figures for the last quarter show a significant increase in online purchases. This growth is mainly due to our new marketing campaign, which focused heavily on social media advertising.","question":"What caused the sales increase?","options":["Better product quality.","A new marketing strategy.","Lower prices.","More physical stores."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage suggests that creating and following a weekly schedule will improve performance and reduce stress.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Students often struggle with time management when balancing studies and part-time jobs. Creating a weekly schedule and sticking to it can significantly improve their academic performance and reduce stress.","question":"What is the main advice for students?","options":["Find a better job.","Study less often.","Organize their time.","Avoid part-time work."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the project aims to reduce congestion and make commuting \"more efficient for travelers.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new high-speed rail line will connect the city center to the airport, cutting travel time by half. This project aims to reduce traffic congestion and make commuting more efficient for travelers.","question":"What is the purpose of the new rail line?","options":["To increase ticket sales.","To build more airports.","To improve travel efficiency.","To create new jobs."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage explains that nature helps people relax, clear their minds, and reduces anxiety, improving mood.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Many people find that spending time in nature helps them relax and clear their minds. Even a short walk in a park can reduce feelings of anxiety and improve overall mood.","question":"What is the benefit of being in nature?","options":["It helps people sleep.","It makes people healthier.","It improves mental well-being.","It encourages exercise."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that \"Regular practice, even for short periods, is key to making steady progress.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Learning a new language can open up many opportunities, from career advancement to deeper cultural understanding. Regular practice, even for short periods, is key to making steady progress.","question":"What is important for language learning?","options":["Traveling abroad.","Consistent practice.","Expensive courses.","Speaking only with natives."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.13","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage clearly states that the change is \"in response to readers' demand for more frequent updates.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The local newspaper announced it will now publish articles online daily, in addition to its weekly print edition. This change is in response to readers' demand for more frequent updates.","question":"Why is the newspaper publishing online daily?","options":["To save money on printing.","To attract new writers.","To meet reader demand.","To compete with TV news."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.reading.14","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the initiative aims to make the city center \"safer and more enjoyable for residents and visitors.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city council approved plans for a new pedestrian zone in the downtown area. This initiative aims to make the city center safer and more enjoyable for residents and visitors.","question":"What is the goal of the new pedestrian zone?","options":["To increase traffic.","To improve safety and enjoyment.","To build new shops.","To reduce parking spaces."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.01","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The present perfect with 'for' is used to describe an action that started in the past and continues up to the present.","kind":"transformation","source":"I started working here five years ago.","instruction":"Use the word 'for'.","answers":["I have worked here for five years.","I have been working here for five years."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.02","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The past simple passive voice is formed with 'was/were' + past participle to describe an action completed in the past.","kind":"transformation","source":"They built this bridge in 1980.","instruction":"Begin with 'This bridge'.","answers":["This bridge was built in 1980."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.03","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Unless' means 'if not', so it introduces a negative condition in a first conditional sentence.","kind":"transformation","source":"If you don't add enough salt, the soup won't taste good.","instruction":"Use the word 'unless'.","answers":["The soup won't taste good unless you add enough salt.","Unless you add enough salt, the soup won't taste good."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.04","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"When reporting a statement, the present continuous tense in direct speech changes to the past continuous tense.","kind":"transformation","source":"The scientist said, 'The polar ice caps are melting quickly.'","instruction":"Begin with 'The scientist said that'.","answers":["The scientist said that the polar ice caps were melting quickly."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.05","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Don't have to' expresses a lack of obligation or necessity, meaning something is not required.","kind":"transformation","source":"It is not necessary for you to bring your own laptop to the meeting.","instruction":"Use the word 'have'.","answers":["You don't have to bring your own laptop to the meeting."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.06","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Used to' describes a past habit or state that no longer happens or is no longer true in the present.","kind":"transformation","source":"When I was younger, I played tennis every weekend, but I don't anymore.","instruction":"Begin with 'I used'.","answers":["I used to play tennis every weekend when I was younger."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.07","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The present perfect with 'since' indicates an action that started in the past and continues up to the present.","kind":"transformation","source":"The last time I saw him was in 2020.","instruction":"Use the word 'since'.","answers":["I haven't seen him since 2020."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.09","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"In reported speech, 'will' changes to 'would' when the reporting verb is in the past.","kind":"transformation","source":"'I will finish my assignment by Friday,' said Maria.","instruction":"Begin with 'Maria said that'.","answers":["Maria said that she would finish her assignment by Friday."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.10","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Used to' describes past habits or states that no longer happen.","kind":"transformation","source":"When I was a child, I often played in the park.","instruction":"Use the word 'used'.","answers":["When I was a child, I used to play in the park."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.11","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The second conditional describes hypothetical situations and their probable results in the present or future.","kind":"transformation","source":"I don't have enough time, so I can't exercise more.","instruction":"Begin with 'If I had'.","answers":["If I had enough time, I would exercise more.","If I had enough time, I could exercise more."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b1.transform.12","level":"B1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Who' is used to introduce a defining relative clause referring to a person.","kind":"transformation","source":"That's the journalist. She wrote the article about the new policy.","instruction":"Use the word 'who'.","answers":["That's the journalist who wrote the article about the new policy."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'relocate operations' is the most appropriate and formal collocation for moving a business's activities to a new place.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company decided to ___ its operations to a new, larger facility to accommodate growth.","options":["move","shift","transfer","relocate"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'adopt an approach' is the standard and most idiomatic collocation for taking on a particular way of thinking or dealing with something.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Students are encouraged to ___ a critical approach to the information they find online.","options":["take","adopt","make","hold"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Grounded' is the specific and idiomatic term used when flights are prevented from taking off due to adverse conditions.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Due to heavy fog, all flights were ___ for several hours, causing significant delays.","options":["stopped","held","grounded","paused"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Broaden your perspective' is the most idiomatic and common collocation for increasing one's understanding or range of views.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Learning a new language can ___ your perspective on different cultures.","options":["widen","expand","broaden","stretch"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.13","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Secure' implies successfully acquiring something that was difficult to get, which fits an 'exclusive interview' with a reclusive person.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The journalist managed to ___ an exclusive interview with the reclusive artist.","options":["get","obtain","secure","achieve"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.14","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Implement' is the correct term for putting a plan, decision, or policy into effect, such as creating a new zone.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council voted to ___ a new pedestrian zone in the downtown area.","options":["apply","implement","execute","perform"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.15","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To pool resources means to combine them for a common purpose, which is the most idiomatic choice here.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Companies often need to ___ their resources to achieve their goals.","options":["pool","gather","collect","mobilize"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.17","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To make good time is an idiom meaning to travel quickly or make progress faster than expected.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Despite the heavy traffic, we managed to ___ good time to the airport.","options":["make","get","arrive","reach"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.18","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To cultivate a sense means to develop or foster a particular feeling or quality over time.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Regular meditation can help individuals to ___ a sense of inner calm.","options":["cultivate","grow","plant","nurture"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.19","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To bridge barriers means to reduce or eliminate differences and create understanding between groups.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Learning a new language can help you to ___ cultural barriers.","options":["bridge","cross","overcome","connect"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.collocation.20","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To uncover the truth means to find out and reveal facts that were previously hidden or unknown, especially through investigation.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The investigative reporter worked tirelessly to ___ the truth behind the scandal.","options":["uncover","reveal","expose","discover"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.01","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The third conditional main clause requires \"would have + past participle\" for a past hypothetical outcome.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If the research team had followed the protocol, the experiment would succeed.","options":["If the research team","had followed the protocol,","the experiment would","succeed."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.02","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Be used to\" is followed by a gerund (-ing form) to describe a familiar habit or state.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"She is used to wake up early for her commute in the city.","options":["She is used to","wake up early","for her commute","in the city."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Whose\" is the possessive relative pronoun needed here, not the contraction \"who's\" (who is/has).","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The artist, who's latest exhibition received critical acclaim, will speak tonight.","options":["The artist,","who's latest exhibition","received critical acclaim,","will speak tonight."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"Modal perfects for past deductions use \"modal + have + past participle.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"He must has forgotten about the meeting, as he didn't show up.","options":["He must","has forgotten","about the meeting,","as he didn't show up."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.06","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Unless\" already implies a negative condition, so a negative verb in the \"unless\" clause creates a double negative.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Unless we don't protect these habitats, many species will disappear.","options":["Unless we don't","protect these habitats,","many species","will disappear."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The main clause should use \"would be + -ing\" for a present continuous result of a past condition.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If the startup had secured the funding last month, they would have been expanding their operations now.","options":["If the startup had secured","the funding last month,","they would have been","expanding their operations now."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The subject of the participle clause \"Walking\" must be the same as the subject of the main clause, which is missing here.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Walking through the dense forest, the sound of a waterfall could be heard in the distance.","options":["Walking through the dense forest,","the sound of a waterfall","could be heard in","the distance."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The verb \"deny\" is typically followed by a gerund (-ing form) when reporting an action.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The news anchor reported that the mayor had refused to comment on the allegations, denying to have received any bribes.","options":["The news anchor reported","that the mayor had refused","to comment on the allegations,","denying to have received any bribes."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Should have felt\" expresses regret or unfulfilled obligation, whereas \"must have felt\" is used for a strong deduction about a past event.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The therapist suggested that the patient should have felt much better after the session.","options":["The therapist suggested","that the patient should have","felt much better","after the session."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The phrase \"get used to\" is always followed by a gerund (-ing form) or a noun.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"After moving to a new country, it took her a while to get used to speak a different language daily.","options":["After moving to a new country,","it took her a while","to get used to","speak a different language daily."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.error.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In a non-defining relative clause, \"which\" is used to refer to things, not \"that\".","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council approved the new pedestrian zone, that will significantly reduce traffic in the downtown area.","options":["The city council approved","the new pedestrian zone,","that will significantly reduce","traffic in the downtown area."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.01","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"This mixed conditional uses a past perfect in the 'if' clause and 'would + base verb' in the main clause to link a past condition to a present result.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If the researchers ___ more funding last year, they would be much closer to a breakthrough now.","answers":[["had received"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.02","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The future perfect tense is used to describe an action that will be completed before a specific point in the future.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"By the time the new high-speed rail line opens next year, the city ___ its entire public transport system.","answers":[["will have upgraded"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.03","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Had better' is used to give strong advice or a warning about what should be done.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"You ___ submit your project proposal by Friday, or you'll miss the deadline for this quarter.","answers":[["had better","must"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause indicates an action completed before the main verb's action.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ the dense forest, the explorers eventually reached a hidden waterfall.","answers":[["Having navigated","Through"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Must have' is used to express a strong deduction or certainty about something that happened in the past.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The painting is so intricate and detailed; it ___ taken the artist months to complete.","answers":[["must have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.06","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The past perfect passive is used to describe an action that was completed before another past action, with the focus on the action itself.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The new recipe for the dessert ___ by the head chef for weeks before it was added to the menu.","answers":[["had been tested"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests the past perfect in the 'if' clause of a third conditional sentence, referring to a hypothetical past situation.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"If the flight ___ delayed, we would have arrived on time for the conference.","answers":[["had not been","hadn't been"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests the future perfect tense, indicating an action that will be finished at a specific point in the future.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"By the end of this semester, she ___ completed all her core modules.","answers":[["will have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests the phrase 'get used to' to describe the process of becoming accustomed to something.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"It took a while, but residents are finally ___ the constant noise from the new construction site.","answers":[["getting used to"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests the use of 'must not have' for a strong deduction about something that probably didn't happen in the past.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"He looks exhausted; he ___ slept at all last night.","answers":[["must not have","mustn't have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests a common reporting verb used to convey information or an opinion.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The professor ___ that learning a new language requires consistent practice.","answers":[["stated","emphasized"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.gap.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"Tests conditional linkers that mean 'only if' or 'on the condition that'.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"You can access the premium features ___ you subscribe to the annual plan.","answers":[["as long as","provided that","providing that"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.01","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The third conditional describes a hypothetical past situation and its past consequence, using \"if + past perfect\" and \"would/might/could have + past participle\".","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If I ___ more thoroughly for the interview, I might have gotten the project manager position.","options":["had prepared","prepared","would prepare","have prepared"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.02","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"A present participle clause (\"-ing\" form) can describe an action that happens at the same time as or immediately after the main verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The research team published their findings, ___ a significant breakthrough in renewable energy.","options":["marking","marked","to mark","having marked"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.03","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Have/get something done\" is used to describe arranging for someone else to do a service for you, using \"have/get + object + past participle\".","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council decided to ___ the historic town hall completely by next year.","options":["have renovated","have it renovated","get renovate","get renovating"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Get used to\" means to become accustomed to something, indicating a process of adaptation.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It took me a while to ___ the spicy flavors of the local cuisine, but now I really enjoy them.","options":["get used to","be used to","used to","get use to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Must have\" is used to express a strong logical deduction or certainty about a past event.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The artist's use of light in this painting is extraordinary; she ___ spent years perfecting her technique.","options":["must have","might have","can't have","should have"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.06","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The future perfect tense describes an action that will be completed by a specific point in the future.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"By the end of this season, the marathon runner ___ over 5,000 kilometers in training.","options":["will have run","will be running","has run","will run"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"This mixed conditional describes how a hypothetical past action (\"if + past perfect\") affects a present result (\"would + base verb\").","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If the company ___ more in research and development years ago, it would be a market leader now.","options":["had invested","invested","would invest","has invested"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Provided that\" introduces a condition that must be met for something else to happen.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We can protect endangered species, ___ we implement stricter conservation laws.","options":["provided that","unless","despite","whereas"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The third conditional uses \"had + past participle\" in the if-clause to talk about hypothetical past situations.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"If the train ___ on time, we wouldn't have missed our connection to the capital.","options":["had arrived","would arrive","arrived","has arrived"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The future perfect \"will have + past participle\" describes an action completed before a specific future time.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"By the time she graduates next year, Sarah ___ five years studying abroad.","options":["will have spent","will spend","has spent","would spend"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Got used to\" correctly expresses the process of becoming accustomed to something in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It took a while, but I finally ___ the constant noise of living downtown.","options":["got used to","am used to","used to","get used to"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Whose\" is the possessive relative pronoun used to refer to the journalist's work in a non-defining clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The investigative journalist, ___ work exposed the scandal, received a prestigious award.","options":["whose","who's","which","that"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.13","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Can't have + past participle\" expresses a strong belief that something was impossible in the past.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"He looks exhausted; he ___ slept well last night.","options":["can't have","mustn't have","shouldn't have","wouldn't have"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.14","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Provided that\" introduces a condition that must be met for the main clause to be true.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company will consider the merger, ___ the terms of the agreement are favorable.","options":["provided that","unless","despite","even if"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.15","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Suggested\" is the correct reporting verb followed by a \"that-clause\" or a gerund to propose an idea.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"My sister ___ that we should try a new restaurant for dinner tonight.","options":["suggested","told","said","explained"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.grammar.16","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Being fluent\" is a present participle clause that provides a reason or explanation for the main clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ fluent in three languages, Maria found it easy to adapt to her new role as an international liaison.","options":["Being","To be","Having been","She was"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.03","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The causative structure 'have something done' means someone else performs an action for the subject.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["They","are","having","their","new","office","designed","by","a","local","architect."],"distractors":["make","to"]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause provides extra information about a noun and is separated by commas.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","restaurant,","which","opened","last","month,","serves","excellent","seafood."],"distractors":["that","and"]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"'Must have' is used to express a strong deduction or certainty about a past event.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["He","must","have","practiced","for","months","to","play","that","piece","so","well."],"distractors":["should","could"]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.06","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"'Get used to' describes the process of becoming accustomed to something new.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["It","took","her","a","while","to","get","used","to","the","intense","training."],"distractors":["being","for"]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The third conditional uses \"if + past perfect\" and \"would have + past participle\" to describe hypothetical past situations.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["If","she","had","booked","earlier,","she","would","have","found","a","cheaper","flight."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The future perfect describes an action that will be finished before a specific point in the future.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["By","next","year,","he","will","have","completed","his","master's","degree."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"Be used to\" means being accustomed to something and is followed by a noun or a gerund.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Residents","are","used","to","the","noise","from","the","new","construction","site."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause provides extra information about a noun and is separated by commas.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","article,","which","was","published","yesterday,","received","widespread","praise."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"Must have + past participle\" expresses a strong deduction or certainty about a past event.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","must","have","been","exhausted","after","working","all","night."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.order.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The reporting verb \"suggest\" is followed by a \"that\" clause with a base verb or a gerund.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","manager","suggested","that","we","review","the","budget","again."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.01","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states employers value 'soft skills like communication and teamwork' and candidates who 'can adapt quickly and collaborate effectively.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The modern workplace increasingly values soft skills like communication and teamwork over purely technical expertise. Employers seek candidates who can adapt quickly and collaborate effectively on diverse projects.","question":"What do employers prioritize in today's job market?","options":["Strict technical knowledge.","Ability to work alone.","Interpersonal and adaptive skills.","Long years of experience."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.02","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states gut bacteria 'play a crucial role in overall health, influencing everything from digestion to mood.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Recent studies suggest that gut bacteria play a crucial role in overall health, influencing everything from digestion to mood. This area of research is rapidly expanding, promising new therapeutic approaches.","question":"What is the main point about gut bacteria?","options":["They only affect digestion.","They are a new discovery.","They have broad health impacts.","They require new therapies."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.03","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states green infrastructure is used 'to combat climate change effects' and 'help manage stormwater and reduce urban heat.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Urban planners are exploring 'green infrastructure' solutions, such as rooftop gardens and permeable pavements, to combat climate change effects. These designs help manage stormwater and reduce urban heat.","question":"What is a purpose of green infrastructure?","options":["To make cities look better.","To fight climate change impacts.","To increase building height.","To create more public spaces."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states fermentation 'Beyond extending shelf life, it enhances flavor and can boost nutritional value.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Fermentation, an ancient food preservation technique, is experiencing a resurgence. Beyond extending shelf life, it enhances flavor and can boost nutritional value, making foods like kimchi and kombucha popular.","question":"What is a benefit of fermentation?","options":["It makes food last longer.","It only applies to new foods.","It reduces food's flavor.","It is a modern invention."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states street art is 'now gaining recognition in the art world' and transforms spaces into 'open-air galleries.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Street art, once dismissed as vandalism, is now gaining recognition in the art world. Its vibrant expressions often reflect social commentary, transforming public spaces into open-air galleries for all to enjoy.","question":"How is street art viewed now?","options":["As a form of vandalism.","As a respected art form.","As a private collection.","As a temporary trend."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.06","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states HIIT 'has become popular for its efficiency' and improves health 'in less time.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"High-intensity interval training (HIIT) has become popular for its efficiency. Short bursts of intense exercise followed by brief recovery periods can improve cardiovascular health in less time than traditional workouts.","question":"Why is HIIT popular?","options":["It is less intense.","It takes a long time.","It is very efficient.","It only builds muscle."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states deforestation is 'threatening biodiversity and contributing to climate change.'","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Deforestation in rainforests continues at an alarming rate, threatening biodiversity and contributing to climate change. Conservation efforts are crucial to protect these vital ecosystems for future generations.","question":"What is a consequence of deforestation?","options":["Increased biodiversity.","Improved climate.","Loss of species and climate impact.","New forest growth."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states the company 'faced significant challenges in scaling its production' and 'ability to meet demand was severely tested'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Despite strong sales, the tech startup faced significant challenges in scaling its production. Investor confidence remained high, but the company's ability to meet demand was severely tested by supply chain disruptions.","question":"What was the main challenge for the startup?","options":["Lack of investor confidence.","Difficulty increasing production.","Weak product sales.","Too much market demand."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage highlights 'flexibility allows professionals to upskill without interrupting their careers'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Many universities now offer online courses, making higher education accessible to a wider audience. This flexibility allows professionals to upskill without interrupting their careers, fostering continuous personal development.","question":"What is a key benefit of online university courses?","options":["They are less expensive than traditional courses.","They guarantee career advancement.","They offer flexible learning for professionals.","They replace traditional campus experiences."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The program was introduced 'to reduce traffic congestion and promote healthier commuting options'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city council introduced a new bike-sharing program to reduce traffic congestion and promote healthier commuting options. The initiative aims to decrease reliance on private cars for short urban trips.","question":"What is a primary goal of the bike-sharing program?","options":["To increase tourism revenue.","To encourage car ownership.","To lessen traffic and promote health.","To expand public transport routes."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that 'Focusing on the present moment can reduce anxiety and improve overall mental clarity'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Mindfulness practices, such as meditation, are increasingly recommended for managing daily stress. Focusing on the present moment can reduce anxiety and improve overall mental clarity, leading to better emotional regulation.","question":"What is suggested to improve mental clarity?","options":["Avoiding all stressful situations.","Engaging in mindfulness practices.","Ignoring emotional regulation.","Increasing daily physical activity."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states that learning a second language 'often enhances cognitive abilities, including problem-solving skills and multitasking'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Learning a second language often enhances cognitive abilities, including problem-solving skills and multitasking. Bilingual individuals frequently demonstrate improved memory and a greater capacity for abstract thought.","question":"What does the passage suggest about learning a second language?","options":["It is only beneficial for young children.","It can improve various cognitive skills.","It makes abstract thought more difficult.","It primarily helps with social interaction."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.13","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states it 'plays a crucial role in holding powerful institutions accountable'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Investigative journalism plays a crucial role in holding powerful institutions accountable. By uncovering hidden truths and exposing corruption, it serves the public interest and strengthens democratic processes.","question":"What is a key function of investigative journalism?","options":["To entertain the public with dramatic stories.","To promote specific political agendas.","To hold powerful groups responsible.","To report only on positive developments."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.reading.14","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage lists benefits like 'improve air quality, reduce the urban heat island effect, and provide residents with vital areas for recreation'.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Integrating green spaces into urban design offers numerous benefits. Parks and gardens improve air quality, reduce the urban heat island effect, and provide residents with vital areas for recreation and relaxation.","question":"What is a main advantage of urban green spaces?","options":["They increase property values significantly.","They attract more tourists to the city.","They enhance environmental quality and recreation.","They require extensive maintenance budgets."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.01","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"This mixed conditional links a past unreal condition to a present unreal result, using \"if + past perfect\" and \"would + base verb\".","kind":"transformation","source":"Because she didn't prepare for the interview, she isn't working at the company now.","instruction":"Use the word 'IF'.","answers":["If she had prepared for the interview, she would be working at the company now."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.02","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"A present participle clause can replace a relative clause to condense information, showing simultaneous action or cause.","kind":"transformation","source":"The scientists, who were hoping to find a cure, conducted extensive research.","instruction":"Use the word 'HOPING'.","answers":["Hoping to find a cure, the scientists conducted extensive research."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.03","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Reporting verbs like 'announced' require changes to pronouns, tenses, and time expressions in reported speech.","kind":"transformation","source":"The architect said, 'I will present the new design next week.'","instruction":"Begin with 'The architect announced that...'","answers":["The architect announced that he would present the new design the following week."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.04","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"\"Must have + past participle\" expresses a strong deduction or certainty about a past event.","kind":"transformation","source":"I'm sure she left her keys at the restaurant because they aren't here.","instruction":"Use the word 'MUST'.","answers":["She must have left her keys at the restaurant because they aren't here."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.05","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"\"Had better\" is used to give strong advice or a warning about what should be done.","kind":"transformation","source":"It would be a good idea for us to start training soon if we want to win the race.","instruction":"Use the word 'BETTER'.","answers":["We had better start training soon if we want to win the race."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.07","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The causative structure 'had something done' indicates that the subject arranged for someone else to perform the action.","kind":"transformation","source":"The city council hired a company to renovate the old market square.","instruction":"Use the word 'HAD'.","answers":["The city council had the old market square renovated.","The city council HAD the old market square renovated by a company."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.08","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'As long as' introduces a condition that must be met for the main clause to be true, similar to 'provided that'.","kind":"transformation","source":"You can borrow my notes, but you must return them by Friday.","instruction":"Begin with 'AS LONG AS'.","answers":["As long as you return them by Friday, you can borrow my notes."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.09","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause using 'having + past participle' shows an action completed before another action.","kind":"transformation","source":"Because she had finished her article, the journalist went home early.","instruction":"Begin with 'HAVING'.","answers":["Having finished her article, the journalist went home early."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.10","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Can't have + past participle' expresses a strong belief that something was impossible in the past.","kind":"transformation","source":"I'm sure she didn't hear the announcement; she looked completely surprised.","instruction":"Use the word 'CAN'T'.","answers":["She can't have heard the announcement; she looked completely surprised."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.11","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The reporting verb 'offered' is followed by an infinitive to report a promise or willingness to do something.","kind":"transformation","source":"\"I will help you with your project tomorrow,\" said my brother.","instruction":"Use the word 'OFFERED'.","answers":["My brother offered to help me with my project the next day."]},{"id":"ex.placement.b2.transform.12","level":"B2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'It's time' followed by a past simple verb expresses that something should happen now or soon.","kind":"transformation","source":"I think we should leave for the airport now.","instruction":"Begin with 'IT'S TIME'.","answers":["It's time we left for the airport."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Mitigate risks\" is the most precise and formal collocation for reducing the severity or impact of potential problems.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new project manager was tasked with identifying potential risks and developing strategies to ___ them.","options":["mitigate","lessen","soften","ease"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Environmentally conscious\" is the standard collocation to describe design that considers its impact on nature and sustainability.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council approved the construction of a new cultural center, praising its innovative and environmentally ___ design.","options":["conscious","aware","mindful","sensitive"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Evoke\" is the most appropriate verb for bringing a feeling, memory, or image into the mind or consciousness.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The artist's latest exhibition aims to ___ a sense of nostalgia for a bygone era through its use of sepia tones and vintage imagery.","options":["evoke","summon","recall","arouse"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.06","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Pose a challenge\" is a common and formal collocation meaning to create or present a difficulty or problem.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"While artificial intelligence offers immense potential, ethical concerns about its widespread adoption continue to ___ a significant challenge.","options":["pose","present","create","offer"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"\"Detrimental\" is a formal adjective meaning causing harm or damage, often used in academic and environmental contexts.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The report highlighted the urgent need to address the ___ effects of climate change on coastal ecosystems.","options":["detrimental","damaging","harmful","adverse"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'yield returns' is the most idiomatic and precise collocation when referring to financial profits or benefits from an investment.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company's recent acquisition is expected to ___ significant returns for shareholders.","options":["yield","provide","generate","offer"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.09","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'adopt a perspective' means to consciously take on a particular viewpoint or way of thinking, which is the most appropriate collocation here.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The professor encouraged students to ___ a critical perspective on the research findings.","options":["adopt","take","assume","hold"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.10","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Flights are 'grounded' when they are prevented from taking off, typically due to safety concerns, weather, or mechanical issues.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Due to the unexpected heavy snowfall, all flights were ___ indefinitely.","options":["grounded","stopped","halted","suspended"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.11","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'mitigate' means to make something less severe, serious, or painful, which is a formal and precise choice for effects of stress.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Regular exercise can help to ___ the effects of stress on the body.","options":["mitigate","lessen","reduce","soften"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.12","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'internalize' a system means to make it an integral part of one's own thought processes or behavior, often unconsciously.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The linguist argued that language acquisition is not merely about memorizing words but about ___ a complex system of rules.","options":["internalizing","absorbing","understanding","grasping"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.13","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'withhold' an article means to intentionally keep it from being published or released, often due to concerns about its content.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The editor decided to ___ the controversial article, fearing it might provoke a strong public backlash.","options":["withhold","suppress","censor","restrain"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.collocation.14","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"To 'strike a balance' is the idiomatic collocation meaning to find a satisfactory compromise between two opposing things.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new zoning regulations aim to ___ a balance between commercial development and residential needs.","options":["strike","achieve","find","create"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"Negative adverbials at the start of a sentence require inversion of the subject and auxiliary verb, so 'does a discovery have' is correct.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Rarely a discovery has such a profound impact on the scientific community.","options":["Rarely a discovery","has such a profound","impact on the","scientific community."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"After verbs like 'insist' expressing a demand, the subjunctive form 'submit' is used for the dependent clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The manager insisted that every team member submits their report by Friday morning.","options":["The manager insisted","that every team member","submits their report","by Friday morning."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The noun form 'construction' is required here, not the gerund 'constructing', to refer to the act itself.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The constructing of the new pedestrian bridge faced several delays due to bad weather.","options":["The constructing of","the new pedestrian bridge","faced several delays","due to bad weather."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In an inverted conditional, 'were' is followed by the subject and then 'to' + base verb, making 'Were you to need' correct.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Were you need further technical assistance, please consult the online troubleshooting guide.","options":["Were you need","further technical assistance,","please consult the","online troubleshooting guide."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"\"Not only\" at the beginning of a sentence requires inversion of the subject and auxiliary verb.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Not only the company announced record profits, but it also expanded into new markets.","options":["Not only the company","announced record profits,","but it also expanded","into new markets."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In an inverted conditional starting with \"Had I,\" the main verb should be in the past participle form.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Had I knew about the scholarship, I would have applied sooner.","options":["Had I knew about","the scholarship, I","would have applied","sooner."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.09","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"After expressions like \"it is crucial that,\" the subjunctive form \"be\" is used for all subjects.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It is crucial that every patient is given adequate support throughout their recovery.","options":["It is crucial that","every patient is given","adequate support","throughout their recovery."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.10","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The structure \"is expected to be completed\" is required for a passive infinitive.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new high-speed train is expected to complete by next year, significantly reducing travel times.","options":["The new high-speed train","is expected to complete","by next year, significantly","reducing travel times."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.error.12","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The phrase \"considered that\" does not require \"as\" when introducing a clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It is generally considered as that the nuances of a language are best learned through immersion.","options":["It is generally considered","as that the nuances","of a language are best","learned through immersion."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"This structure uses inversion (auxiliary verb before subject) after a negative adverbial like 'never before' for emphasis.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"Never before ___ scientists observed such a phenomenon in the deep ocean.","answers":[["have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Should' can replace 'if' in a conditional clause, especially in formal contexts, leading to inversion.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ you require further assistance with your application, please do not hesitate to contact our HR department.","answers":[["Should"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The impersonal passive 'It is believed that...' is used to report general opinions or facts without specifying the source.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"It is widely ___ that the city's original design was inspired by ancient Roman layouts, though direct evidence is scarce.","answers":[["believed"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.04","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause indicates an action completed before the main verb's action, often showing cause or sequence.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ prepared all the ingredients, the chef began to assemble the complex multi-course meal.","answers":[["Having"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The subjunctive 'be' is used after expressions of necessity, importance, or recommendation, especially in formal contexts.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"It is crucial that the new software ___ thoroughly tested before its public release to avoid any critical bugs.","answers":[["be"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.06","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"'Would appear' is a common hedging phrase used to express an opinion or observation with caution or less certainty.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"It ___ appear that the recent climate changes are having a significant impact on local wildlife populations.","answers":[["would"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause indicates an action completed before the main verb's action, showing cause or sequence.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ all the required courses, Sarah was finally able to graduate with honors.","answers":[["Having completed"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.09","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The subjunctive mood is used after expressions of importance or necessity, requiring the base form of the verb.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"It is crucial that every patient ___ access to mental health resources.","answers":[["have"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.10","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"This inverted conditional structure replaces 'If it had not been for' to express a hypothetical past condition and result.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ for the sudden storm, our flight would have departed on time.","answers":[["Had it not been"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.gap.11","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"This advanced passive structure reports information or opinions, often used in formal or journalistic contexts.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The new policy ___ to have been leaked to the press by an anonymous source.","answers":[["is said"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The negative adverbial 'Never before' requires inversion of the auxiliary verb and subject, and 'has been' correctly forms the present perfect passive.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Never before ___ such a complex algorithm developed to model climate change.","options":["has been","was","had","is"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause ('Having gathered') indicates an action completed before the main verb's action, providing a concise way to link events.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ all the necessary data, the analyst began to prepare her presentation.","options":["Having gathered","She gathered","To gather","Gathered"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive 'be' is used after verbs like 'recommend' to express a suggestion or requirement, regardless of tense.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council recommended that the new building ___ designed to incorporate green spaces.","options":["be","is","was","should be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.04","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase 'would seem to be' is a common hedging expression, indicating a cautious conclusion while acknowledging uncertainty.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The recent decline in bee populations ___ linked to pesticide use, though more research is needed.","options":["would seem to be","is definitely","must be","has been proven"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The advanced passive structure 'is thought to have been created' correctly conveys a general belief about a past action.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The ancient sculpture ___ to have been created by an unknown master from the Hellenistic period.","options":["is thought","thought","was thinking","has thought"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.06","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The inverted conditional 'Should any issues arise' is a formal way to express a first conditional without using 'if'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ any technical issues arise during the software update, please contact support immediately.","options":["Should","If","Were","Had"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Nominalisation transforms the verb 'utilize' into the noun 'utilization', which fits grammatically after the possessive 'chef's'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The chef's ___ of fresh, local ingredients was key to the dish's success.","options":["utilization","to utilize","utilizing","utilized"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Needn't have booked' correctly expresses that an action was performed but was unnecessary in hindsight.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"We ___ booked the tennis court in advance; it was completely empty when we arrived.","options":["needn't have","didn't need","mustn't have","shouldn't have"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.09","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"When a negative adverbial like 'Not only' begins a sentence, the subject and auxiliary verb are inverted, similar to a question structure.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Not only ___ the journalist uncover the scandal, but she also exposed the systemic corruption behind it.","options":["did","has","was","is"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.10","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"This inverted conditional structure replaces 'If it had not been for' to express a past hypothetical situation and its consequence.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ for the city council's swift action, the historic district would have been demolished to make way for new high-rises.","options":["Had it not been","Were it not","Should it not be","If it wasn't"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.11","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'What' introduces a noun clause that functions as the subject of the sentence, emphasizing the information that follows.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ truly surprised me was how quickly the therapy helped him overcome his anxiety.","options":["What","That","Which","It"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.12","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"This impersonal passive structure is used to report general opinions or beliefs about a subject, with the adverb typically preceding the participle.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The new curriculum ___ to be highly effective in improving student engagement.","options":["is widely believed","widely believes","widely believing","is believed widely"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.13","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The perfect participle clause 'Having obtained' shows that the action of obtaining permits was completed before the company proceeded.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ all the necessary permits, the company proceeded with the construction of its new headquarters.","options":["Having obtained","To obtain","Obtaining","Obtained"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.14","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive 'express' is used after expressions of necessity, importance, or recommendation, regardless of the subject's number or person.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It is crucial that every family member ___ their concerns openly during the mediation session.","options":["express","expresses","expressed","to express"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.15","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Might well be' is a hedging expression that indicates a strong possibility or probability without stating it as a certainty.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The researcher suggested that the observed linguistic shift ___ due to increased digital communication.","options":["might well be","is definitely","must be","will be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.grammar.16","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Implementation' is the noun form of the verb 'implement,' used here to refer to the act of putting the rail line into effect.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The ___ of the new high-speed rail line is expected to significantly reduce travel times between major cities.","options":["implementation","implement","implementing","implemented"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Inversion is used after negative adverbials like 'never before' for emphasis, placing the auxiliary verb before the subject.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Never","before","had","such","a","complex","algorithm","been","developed","for","climate","modeling."],"distractors":["even"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Inverted conditionals like 'Should you require' replace 'If you should require' for a more formal tone.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Should","you","require","further","assistance,","please","contact","our","support","team."],"distractors":["might"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"A 'what-cleft' sentence emphasizes a particular piece of information by placing it after 'what' and a form of 'be'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["What","truly","impressed","the","critics","was","the","artist's","innovative","use","of","light."],"distractors":["that"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.04","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause ('Having completed') indicates an action that happened before the main verb's action.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Having","completed","their","migration,","the","birds","began","to","build","their","nests."],"distractors":["after"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This advanced passive structure ('is said to have been designed') reports an opinion or belief about a past action.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","new","skyscraper","is","said","to","have","been","designed","by","a","renowned","architect."],"distractors":["must"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The negative adverbial 'Seldom' at the beginning of the sentence requires inversion of the auxiliary verb 'do' and the subject 'we'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Seldom","do","we","see","such","unbiased","reporting","in","today's","media."],"distractors":["ever"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This is an inverted conditional structure where 'if' is omitted and the auxiliary verb 'Had' is placed before the subject 'the company'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Had","the","company","invested","more","it","would","have","avoided","bankruptcy."],"distractors":["if"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.11","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"The subjunctive form 'be' is correctly used after expressions of necessity or importance, such as 'It is essential that'.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["It","is","essential","that","he","be","present","at","the","family","meeting."],"distractors":["was"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.order.12","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This advanced passive construction uses 'is believed to' to report a general opinion or belief about the therapy's effect.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","new","therapy","is","believed","to","improve","patients'","overall","mood."],"distractors":["they"]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states employees dictate immediate tasks but not strategic direction, indicating limited control, hence an 'illusion' of autonomy.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The modern workplace, increasingly characterized by project-based assignments and flexible hours, often presents an illusion of autonomy. While employees may dictate their immediate tasks, the overarching strategic direction and performance metrics are typically determined by senior management, leaving little room for genuine self-governance in critical decision-making.","question":"What does \"illusion of autonomy\" imply?","options":["Employees have complete control over their work.","Employees only control minor aspects of their work.","Management trusts employees with all decisions.","Autonomy is a new concept in the workplace."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The author acknowledges the findings are 'compelling' but stresses 'considerable caution' and the need for 'further inquiry,' indicating skepticism but openness.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Recent findings suggesting a definitive link between certain dietary compounds and cognitive decline, while compelling, must be interpreted with considerable caution. The studies, often relying on self-reported data and relatively small sample sizes, provide a foundation for further inquiry rather than conclusive evidence. To present them as anything more would be premature.","question":"What is the author's attitude towards the recent findings?","options":["Enthusiastic and fully convinced.","Skeptical but open to further research.","Dismissive of their potential importance.","Indifferent to the implications."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase refers to the preceding description of high-rise structures that prioritize density and economic efficiency.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The rapid urbanization of the past century has led to a proliferation of high-rise structures, often prioritizing density and economic efficiency over aesthetic integration or community well-being. This architectural paradigm, while addressing housing shortages, frequently results in a loss of local character and a diminished sense of place for residents.","question":"What does \"This architectural paradigm\" refer to?","options":["The rapid urbanization of the past century.","The prioritization of density and economic efficiency.","The proliferation of high-rise structures.","The loss of local character and sense of place."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.04","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The claim about improved immune function is qualified by 'potentially' and the need for 'more extensive longitudinal research,' making it hedged.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"While fermentation has long been recognized for its role in food preservation and flavor development, its broader health benefits are still being rigorously investigated. Some studies suggest a positive impact on gut microbiota, potentially leading to improved digestion and immune function, though definitive causal links require more extensive longitudinal research.","question":"Which claim is most hedged in the passage?","options":["Fermentation preserves food and develops flavor.","Fermentation definitely improves digestion.","Fermentation might improve immune function.","Fermentation's health benefits are fully understood."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"By stating contemporary art challenges 'conventional notions of beauty and craftsmanship,' the passage implies traditional art upheld them.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Contemporary art often challenges conventional notions of beauty and craftsmanship, prioritizing conceptual depth and audience engagement over traditional aesthetic appeal. This shift, while sometimes alienating to those accustomed to classical forms, invites a more active and interpretive role from the viewer, transforming passive observation into a dynamic intellectual exchange.","question":"What does the passage imply about traditional art?","options":["It requires active interpretation from the viewer.","It primarily focuses on conceptual depth.","It emphasizes conventional beauty and craftsmanship.","It is often alienating to modern audiences."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.06","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The author expresses concern about 'ethical implications' and calls for 'a more cautious and comprehensive societal dialogue' before full integration.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The promise of artificial intelligence to revolutionize various industries is undeniable, yet the ethical implications of its widespread deployment remain largely unaddressed. While proponents often highlight efficiency gains, the potential for job displacement, algorithmic bias, and privacy erosion warrants a more cautious and comprehensive societal dialogue before full integration.","question":"What is the author's attitude towards AI's full integration?","options":["Unreservedly optimistic.","Enthusiastically supportive.","Cautious and concerned.","Indifferent to its impact."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"Encroachment refers to the gradual intrusion or expansion of human activities, such as agriculture and logging, into natural habitats.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Deforestation in tropical regions continues at an alarming rate, driven primarily by agricultural expansion and logging. This relentless encroachment on natural habitats not only threatens countless species with extinction but also significantly contributes to global climate change by reducing the planet's capacity to absorb carbon dioxide.","question":"What does \"This relentless encroachment\" refer to?","options":["The rapid pace of habitat loss.","The expansion of human activities into natural areas.","The extinction of various animal species.","The planet's reduced ability to absorb carbon."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.22","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase \"shifts the burden of risk from employer to individual\" directly implies that financial uncertainty is transferred to employees.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The contemporary labor market, increasingly characterized by project-based assignments and flexible contracts, often presents a veneer of liberation. However, beneath this surface, many workers find themselves in a precarious state, constantly navigating a landscape devoid of traditional benefits or long-term security, a situation that subtly shifts the burden of risk from employer to individual.","question":"What does the passage imply about modern work arrangements?","options":["They transfer financial uncertainty to employees.","They consistently empower individual workers.","They guarantee more diverse professional experiences.","They are a temporary economic adjustment."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.23","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The author notes that self-reported data \"introduces inherent limitations\" and \"potentially skewing the generalizability of findings.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"Recent studies on cognitive biases in decision-making have often relied heavily on self-reported data, a methodology that, while convenient, introduces inherent limitations. The subjective nature of introspection means that participants may not accurately perceive or articulate their own biases, potentially skewing the generalizability of findings.","question":"What is the author's stance on self-reported data in bias research?","options":["It offers unique insights into personal biases.","It should be entirely avoided in future studies.","It has significant methodological drawbacks.","It is the most reliable method available."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.24","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase \"towering glass and steel structures\" directly precedes and describes \"these monolithic forms.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The rapid urbanization of the past century has often prioritized efficiency and density, leading to the proliferation of towering glass and steel structures. While undeniably functional, these monolithic forms frequently neglect the human scale, contributing to a sense of alienation rather than community within urban cores.","question":"What does \"these monolithic forms\" refer to?","options":["Community-focused designs.","Historical city centers.","Sustainable urban planning.","Modern high-rise buildings."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.25","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states it \"signals a deeper societal yearning for authenticity and connection to the origins of our food.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The burgeoning interest in hyper-local sourcing and forgotten culinary traditions represents more than a fleeting trend; it signals a deeper societal yearning for authenticity and connection to the origins of our food. This movement challenges the industrial food system's homogenizing effects, advocating for diversity and regional distinctiveness.","question":"What does the passage suggest about the \"hyper-local sourcing\" movement?","options":["It promotes global culinary uniformity.","It aims to reduce food production costs.","It reflects a desire for genuine food experiences.","It is a temporary fashion in gastronomy."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.26","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage acknowledges both \"democratized access\" (benefit) and the difficulty for \"profound pieces to gain recognition\" (challenge).","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"While digital platforms have democratized access to art, allowing countless creators to share their work globally, this accessibility sometimes comes at a cost. The sheer volume of content can overwhelm audiences, making it difficult for truly innovative or profound pieces to gain the recognition they deserve amidst the noise.","question":"What is the author's perspective on digital art platforms?","options":["They universally enhance artist recognition.","They present both benefits and challenges.","They primarily diminish artistic quality.","They are irrelevant to traditional art forms."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.27","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage states genetic predispositions \"undoubtedly play a role\" but their influence is \"arguably less deterministic.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The pursuit of peak athletic performance often involves a complex interplay of physiological conditioning, psychological resilience, and strategic coaching. While genetic predispositions undoubtedly play a role, their influence is arguably less deterministic than the sustained, deliberate practice and mental fortitude exhibited by elite athletes.","question":"What does the passage imply about genetic factors in athletic success?","options":["They are entirely negligible compared to training.","They are more significant than mental fortitude.","They are the primary determinant of elite performance.","They contribute, but are not the sole deciding factor."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.reading.28","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"This pervasive integration\" refers to the \"increasing reliance on algorithms to curate everything from news feeds to social interactions.\"","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The increasing reliance on algorithms to curate everything from news feeds to social interactions has profound implications for individual autonomy and societal discourse. This pervasive integration, while often designed for convenience, subtly shapes perceptions and limits exposure to diverse viewpoints, raising concerns about echo chambers.","question":"What does \"This pervasive integration\" refer to?","options":["The creation of echo chambers.","The shaping of individual perceptions.","The design of convenient algorithms.","The widespread use of algorithms in daily life."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.01","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"When 'Not until' begins a sentence, the auxiliary verb and subject are inverted to form a question-like structure.","kind":"transformation","source":"The researchers only realized the full implications of their discovery after years of further study.","instruction":"Begin with 'Not until'.","answers":["Not until years of further study did the researchers realize the full implications of their discovery."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.02","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Nominalisation transforms the verb 'regenerate' into the noun 'regeneration' to create a more formal and concise sentence.","kind":"transformation","source":"The city council decided to regenerate the old industrial district, which led to a significant improvement in the area.","instruction":"Use the word 'regeneration'.","answers":["The city council's decision for the regeneration of the old industrial district led to a significant improvement in the area.","The city council decided on the regeneration of the old industrial district, leading to a significant improvement in the area."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.03","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"This advanced passive structure uses 'is considered to be' to report general opinion about someone or something.","kind":"transformation","source":"People generally consider Dr. Elena Petrova to be one of the most influential leaders in her field.","instruction":"Begin with 'Dr. Elena Petrova'.","answers":["Dr. Elena Petrova is generally considered to be one of the most influential leaders in her field."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.04","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause indicates an action completed before another action, making the sentence more concise and formal.","kind":"transformation","source":"Because they had completed the extensive environmental impact assessment, the team was able to proceed with the conservation project.","instruction":"Begin with 'Having'.","answers":["Having completed the extensive environmental impact assessment, the team was able to proceed with the conservation project."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.05","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The subjunctive mood uses the base form of the verb (be) after expressions of importance, necessity, or recommendation.","kind":"transformation","source":"It is essential that every participant attends the workshop on time.","instruction":"Begin with 'It is essential that every participant'.","answers":["It is essential that every participant be present at the workshop on time.","It is essential that every participant attend the workshop on time."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.07","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Negative adverbials like 'only after' at the beginning of a sentence require inversion of the subject and auxiliary verb.","kind":"transformation","source":"The city council rarely considered the environmental impact until residents protested.","instruction":"Only after residents protested, ...","answers":["Only after residents protested did the city council consider the environmental impact.","Only after residents protested, did the city council consider the environmental impact."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.08","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The causative structure 'have something done' indicates that someone else performed the action for the subject.","kind":"transformation","source":"The company arranged for a new security system to be installed last month.","instruction":"The company had...","answers":["The company had a new security system installed last month."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.09","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"A perfect participle clause ('having + past participle') can replace a 'because' clause to show a completed action preceding another.","kind":"transformation","source":"Because she had completed all her assignments, Sarah felt confident about her final grades.","instruction":"Having...","answers":["Having completed all her assignments, Sarah felt confident about her final grades."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.10","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"After expressions of necessity or importance like 'it is essential that', the subjunctive mood uses the base form of the verb.","kind":"transformation","source":"It is essential that every team member understands the importance of mental health breaks.","instruction":"It is essential that every team member...","answers":["It is essential that every team member understand the importance of mental health breaks."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c1.transform.11","level":"C1","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"In a third conditional, 'if' can be omitted and 'had' inverted with the subject to form a more formal conditional clause.","kind":"transformation","source":"If you had booked your flight earlier, you would have saved a significant amount of money.","instruction":"Had you...","answers":["Had you booked your flight earlier, you would have saved a significant amount of money."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"An 'intimation' is an indirect suggestion or hint, fitting the context of an unannounced decision.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The CEO's decision to step down was a complete surprise, as there had been no prior ___ of his intentions.","options":["intimation","insinuation","implication","inference"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Elucidate' means to make something clear or explain it, especially complex information, fitting the context of complex data.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The research team worked tirelessly to ___ the complex data, hoping to uncover a new pattern.","options":["elucidate","illuminate","explicate","clarify"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.05","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"'Imbue' means to inspire or permeate with a feeling or quality, fitting the context of conveying a pervasive mood.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The critic praised the young playwright's ability to ___ a sense of profound melancholy throughout the entire performance.","options":["imbue","instill","infuse","impart"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.15","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Stave off is the most idiomatic phrasal verb used to describe preventing something undesirable, like bankruptcy, from happening.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company implemented drastic cost-cutting measures to ___ bankruptcy during the economic downturn.","options":["ward off","stave off","fend off","keep off"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.16","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Discerning eye is an idiomatic collocation referring to the ability to judge well or perceive subtle differences and qualities.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Her professor praised her for bringing a truly ___ eye to the historical documents, uncovering subtle biases.","options":["observing","discerning","noticing","perceiving"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.18","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"A sense of foreboding is an idiomatic expression for a strong feeling that something bad is about to happen.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"As the storm clouds gathered, a deep sense of ___ settled over the small coastal town.","options":["apprehension","misgiving","dread","foreboding"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.19","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Nuanced understanding is the most precise collocation for grasping the subtle distinctions and complexities of a subject.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Achieving a truly ___ understanding of a foreign language requires years of immersion and dedicated study.","options":["subtle","intricate","nuanced","complex"],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.20","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Unflinching portrayal is an idiomatic collocation for a depiction that does not recoil or hesitate from showing difficult truths.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The documentary offered an ___ portrayal of the war's devastating impact, refusing to shy away from harsh realities.","options":["unwavering","unyielding","unblinking","unflinching"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.collocation.21","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the word that fits best.","explanation":"Urban sprawl is the specific, idiomatic term for the uncontrolled and often disorganized expansion of urban areas into surrounding land.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Critics argue that unchecked ___ has led to a loss of community identity and increased reliance on private vehicles.","options":["city expansion","metropolitan growth","town spread","urban sprawl"],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In inversion with 'so + adjective', the auxiliary verb comes before the subject: 'So challenging was the project'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"So challenging the project was that many team members considered resigning.","options":["So challenging","the project was that","many team members","considered resigning."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In this absolute construction, 'data' is the object of 'recorded', so the passive voice 'having been recorded' is required.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The experiment concluded, all data having meticulously recorded, the team began its analysis.","options":["The experiment concluded,","all data having meticulously recorded,","the team began","its analysis."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.04","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"The verb 'concur' typically takes 'that' before a reported clause: 'concurred that it was a masterpiece'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The director's latest film was lauded by critics, who all concurred it was a masterpiece.","options":["The director's latest film","was lauded by critics,","who all concurred it","was a masterpiece."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.05","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"After 'lest', the mandative subjunctive form of the verb is used, which is the base form ('become') for all subjects.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"They implemented strict conservation measures lest the endangered species becomes extinct.","options":["They implemented strict","conservation measures lest","the endangered species","becomes extinct."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.06","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In inverted conditional sentences with 'had', the negative particle 'not' comes after the subject: 'Had he not been injured'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Had not he been injured, he would have won the championship last year.","options":["Had not he been injured,","he would have won","the championship","last year."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.07","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"“So talented a student was she” is the correct inverted structure for emphasis.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"So talented she was a student that the university offered her a full scholarship.","options":["So talented she was a student","that the university","offered her","a full scholarship."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.08","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"After “lest,” the mandative subjunctive (base form of the verb) or “should” + base form is used, not “would.”","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city implemented strict zoning laws lest uncontrolled development would mar its historic character.","options":["The city implemented","strict zoning laws lest","uncontrolled development","would mar its historic character."],"answer":3},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.09","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"In an absolute construction, the past participle “completed” is needed to show the project was finished.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The project completing, the team celebrated their success with a small gathering.","options":["The project completing,","the team celebrated","their success","with a small gathering."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.10","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"For a past condition with a present result, the main clause should use “would + base verb,” not “would have + past participle.”","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"Had the company invested more in R&D, it would have been a market leader in the current climate.","options":["Had the company invested","more in R&D, it","would have been a market leader","in the current climate."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.error.12","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Which part of the sentence is wrong?","explanation":"“Quite” is not typically used to hedge “believed” in this context; “widely,” “generally,” or “commonly” would be more appropriate.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"It is quite believed that language acquisition is a remarkably complex process.","options":["It is quite believed","that language acquisition","is a remarkably","complex process."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“So intense” is inverted with the verb “was” to emphasize the degree of pressure.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ was the pressure on the team that several members considered resigning.","answers":[["So intense","Such"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive “be” is used after “recommended that” to express a requirement or suggestion.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The research committee recommended that the new experimental protocol ___ implemented immediately.","answers":[["be"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.04","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"This phrase combines multiple adjectives and nouns to form a dense, descriptive noun phrase.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The city council approved the construction of a ___ designed to revitalize the downtown area.","answers":[["multi-story mixed-use development"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.06","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Lest” introduces a negative purpose clause, meaning “for fear that” or “in order to prevent.”","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The baker added a pinch of salt to the dough, ___ the flavor be too bland.","answers":[["lest"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.07","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“So great” is used with inversion to emphasize the extent of her dedication, followed by a “that” clause.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"So ___ was her dedication to her patients that she often worked through the night without complaint.","answers":[["great"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.08","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Lest” introduces a negative purpose, requiring the subjunctive “be” to express a preventative measure.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The city council approved the new zoning laws, ___ there be any further uncontrolled development in the historic district.","answers":[["lest"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.10","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Tentatively” is an adverb used to hedge a statement, indicating uncertainty or a preliminary nature.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"While the preliminary research ___ suggests a correlation between diet and mood, more extensive studies are needed to confirm causation.","answers":[["tentatively","itself"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.11","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Had it not been for” is an inverted conditional structure equivalent to “If it had not been for.”","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ it not been for the unexpected flight delay, they would have arrived in time for the international conference.","answers":[["Had"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.14","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Lest” introduces a negative purpose clause, meaning “in order to prevent something from happening,” followed by a bare infinitive.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"The city council implemented strict building codes, ___ new constructions compromise the historic character of the district.","answers":[["lest"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.16","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Had it not been for” is an inverted third conditional structure used to express a hypothetical past condition and its consequence.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ for the unexpected blizzard, our flight would have departed on time, and we wouldn't have missed the connection.","answers":[["Had it not been","But for"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.gap.18","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Type the missing word or words.","explanation":"“Strictly speaking” is a stance adverbial phrase used to qualify a statement, indicating precision or a particular interpretation.","kind":"gap_fill","text":"___ speaking, the journalist's exposé, while impactful, relied heavily on anonymous sources, raising questions about its full veracity.","answers":[["Strictly"]]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The inversion 'so + adjective + verb + subject' is used for emphasis, indicating a high degree of the quality described.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"So ___ the performance that the audience erupted in a standing ovation lasting several minutes.","options":["moving was","was moving","it was moving","moving it was"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive 'be' is used after 'lest' to express a negative purpose or to prevent something from happening.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The company policy dictates that all confidential documents ___ securely shredded, lest they fall into the wrong hands.","options":["be","are","should be","must be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.03","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"An absolute construction, consisting of a noun phrase followed by a participle or adjective, provides additional information about the main clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The hikers continued their ascent, ___ the summit still shrouded in a thick, impenetrable fog.","options":["the summit still shrouded","with the summit still shrouded","and the summit was still shrouded","its summit still shrouded"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.04","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Had it not been for' is an inverted conditional structure used to express a hypothetical past condition with a past result.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ for the unexpected discovery of a new catalyst, the experiment would have failed to yield any significant results.","options":["Had it not been","If it wasn't","Were it not","Should it not be"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.07","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Considerable' is a hedging adjective that suggests a significant but not absolute degree of promise, appropriate for cautious scientific reporting.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"While the initial tests of the new quantum processor show ___ promise, further rigorous evaluation is required before mass production.","options":["considerable","undeniable","absolute","certain"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.08","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"'Be that as it may' is a fixed subjunctive phrase meaning 'regardless of the truth of that' or 'even if that is true'.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ it may, the chef insisted that the dish be served immediately to preserve its delicate flavors.","options":["Be that as","As that be","That be as","Be as that"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.09","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"Inversion with \"so\" + adjective + \"be\" + subject emphasizes the degree of the adjective, linking it to the result clause.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ the demand for the new smartphone that the company struggled to keep up with orders.","options":["So great was","Such great was","So was great","Such was great"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.10","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Lest\" introduces a negative purpose clause, indicating a precaution taken to prevent an undesirable outcome.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The city council implemented strict zoning laws, ___ uncontrolled development might overwhelm the existing infrastructure.","options":["lest","otherwise","so that","in case"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.11","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"An absolute construction provides additional information about the main clause, often describing a related circumstance.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The train departed precisely on schedule, ___ the passengers settled into their seats for the long journey ahead.","options":["all luggage stowed","all luggage having stowed","all luggage stowing","all luggage was stowed"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.12","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Tentatively\" is a hedging adverb that indicates the initial suggestion was not firm or conclusive, contrasting with the later findings.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"While the initial report ___ suggested a significant breakthrough, further analysis indicates the findings are somewhat preliminary.","options":["strongly","tentatively","definitively","undoubtedly"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.13","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"This option uses a dense nominal phrase, typical of formal and academic English, to convey complex information concisely.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"The university's ___ is a testament to its commitment to academic excellence.","options":["rigorous curriculum development process","process of developing a rigorous curriculum","development of a rigorous curriculum process","curriculum's rigorous development process"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.grammar.14","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Choose the best answer.","explanation":"This is an inverted mixed conditional, combining a past perfect condition (past action) with a present result.","kind":"multiple_choice","question":"___ more time to reflect on her decision, she wouldn't be feeling so much regret now.","options":["Had she had","If she had","Should she have","Were she to have"],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"So followed by an adjective and inverted subject-verb order emphasizes the degree of complexity.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["So","complex","was","the","data","that","researchers","struggled","to","interpret","it."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"An absolute construction, 'its towers gleaming under the morning sun,' modifies the entire main clause.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","city","skyline,","its","towers","gleaming","under","the","morning","sun,","presented","a","stunning","view."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.03","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Lest introduces a negative purpose, requiring the mandative subjunctive 'be missed' after 'deadlines.'","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","manager","insisted","that","all","reports","be","submitted","promptly","lest","deadlines","be","missed."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.05","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This inverted conditional combines a past perfect condition ('Had... started') with a present result ('might not be endangered now').","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Had","the","conservation","efforts","started","earlier,","the","species","might","not","be","endangered","now."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.07","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"So followed by an adjective and inverted subject-verb order emphasizes the degree of the adjective.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["So","intense","was","the","stress","that","she","almost","quit","."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.10","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Lest introduces a negative purpose, requiring the mandative subjunctive (base form of the verb) in the following clause.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","professor","insisted","that","students","arrive","on","time","lest","they","miss","important","announcements","."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.11","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"Dense nominal style uses a long chain of nouns and adjectives to convey complex information concisely.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","city","council's","recent","decision","regarding","public","transport","infrastructure","development","caused","widespread","debate","."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.13","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This structure uses inversion after \"so + adjective\" at the beginning of a sentence for emphasis.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["So","overwhelming","was","her","anxiety","that","she","found","it","hard","to","focus."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.14","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"Come what may\" is a fixed subjunctive phrase meaning \"whatever happens,\" used to express determination.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Come","what","may",",","the","company","must","adapt","to","the","economic","challenges."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.15","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"An absolute construction, \"The new park having been completed,\" provides additional information about the main clause.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["The","new","park","having","been","completed",",","the","city","celebrated","its","green","initiative."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.17","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"\"Lest\" introduces a negative purpose clause, requiring the mandative subjunctive form \"fail\" (base form of the verb).","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["She","advised","her","son","to","study","diligently","lest","he","fail","the","exam."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.order.18","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Put the words in the right order.","explanation":"This is an inverted mixed conditional, combining a past condition (\"Had the flight not been delayed\") with a present result.","kind":"sentence_building","answer":["Had","the","flight","not","been","delayed",",","they","would","be","here","by","now."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage highlights how the policy, despite being praised, actually led to a negative outcome, implying it failed its stated purpose.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new 'flexi-work' policy, lauded as empowering, often meant staff were simply expected to be 'available' at all hours, blurring work and personal life with remarkable efficiency.","question":"What does the writer imply about the 'flexi-work' policy?","options":["It was a genuine improvement","It failed to meet expectations","It was poorly designed","It was a clever deception"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.03","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The writer uses irony by contrasting the \"eco-friendly\" label with the skyscraper's actual environmental impact, creating a sarcastic tone.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city's 'eco-friendly' skyscraper, a glass and steel monument, required demolishing historic buildings and consumes vast power. A truly sustainable vision.","question":"What is the writer's tone regarding the skyscraper?","options":["Admiring","Sarcastic","Neutral","Concerned"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.05","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase \"merely an expensive exercise in nostalgia\" suggests that despite its modern appearance, the exhibition lacked genuine innovation.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The artist's latest exhibition, hailed as a bold reinterpretation, struck others as merely an expensive exercise in nostalgia, albeit with a contemporary veneer.","question":"What does the writer suggest about the exhibition?","options":["It was universally praised","It lacked originality","It was truly innovative","It was controversial"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.06","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase \"triumph of moderation\" suggests the runner prioritized a steady pace over pushing for a faster, potentially riskier, time.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"After rigorous training, the runner finished the marathon in a time that, while not record-breaking, was respectable, a triumph of moderation for some.","question":"What does the writer imply about the runner's performance?","options":["It was a personal best","It was a cautious effort","It was surprisingly fast","It was a clear victory"],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.10","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"Despite solar panels, the resort's remote location means guests' travel negates its \"eco-friendly\" claim.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The \"eco-friendly\" resort boasted solar panels, yet its remote location necessitated long, fuel-intensive journeys for most guests.","question":"What is ironic about the resort's claim?","options":["Its reliance on local produce.","The high cost of its amenities.","Its overall environmental impact.","The lack of public transport access."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.12","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The resistance of \"nuances of human communication\" to algorithms implies that human interpretation is essential.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The \"universal translator\" remains a sci-fi dream; nuances of human communication resist simple algorithmic solutions.","question":"What does the passage suggest about translation?","options":["It is an easily automated task.","It requires human interpretation.","It is a rapidly advancing field.","It will soon be perfected."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.13","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"\"Unconventional approach\" followed by \"barely met editorial standards\" is a euphemism for criticism, implying flaws.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The editor praised the reporter's \"unconventional approach,\" a polite way of saying the piece barely met editorial standards.","question":"What did the editor subtly imply?","options":["The reporter was highly creative.","The article was groundbreaking.","The work was somewhat flawed.","The reporter needed more freedom."],"answer":2},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.15","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage subtly suggests the 'realignment' disproportionately benefited executives, implying their self-interest was the true purpose.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The company's latest 'strategic realignment' was presented as a necessary step for future growth, though the most visible outcome was a substantial increase in executive bonuses, while other departments faced budget cuts.","question":"What does the passage imply about the realignment's true purpose?","options":["It was primarily for executive benefit.","It aimed to streamline operations.","It ensured long-term company stability.","It was a response to market pressures."],"answer":0},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.16","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage implies the curriculum's practical implementation falls short of its stated innovative goals, focusing on documentation over reflection.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new 'experiential learning' curriculum was lauded for its innovative approach, though early reports indicated students spent more time documenting their experiences than actually reflecting on them.","question":"What does the writer suggest about the new curriculum?","options":["It effectively promotes deep reflection.","Its practical application is flawed.","It is a genuinely groundbreaking reform.","It requires more funding to succeed."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.17","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage uses 'somewhat offset' to imply that the project's celebrated benefits are diminished by its negative consequences.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The new high-speed rail line was celebrated for cutting travel times, a benefit somewhat offset by the extensive land acquisition disputes and the unforeseen ecological impact on local wetlands.","question":"What does the passage hint about the rail project's overall success?","options":["It was an unqualified triumph.","Its benefits came with significant drawbacks.","It faced minor, easily resolved issues.","It primarily benefited the environment."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.18","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The writer's phrasing 'seemed to overlook the complexities' suggests skepticism about the retreat's ability to deliver on its ambitious promise.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The latest 'mindfulness retreat' promised profound inner peace in just three days, a claim that, while appealing, seemed to overlook the complexities of sustained personal growth and emotional regulation.","question":"What does the writer imply about the retreat's promise?","options":["It offers a realistic path to peace.","It oversimplifies personal development.","It is a highly effective therapeutic method.","It caters to a niche audience."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.19","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The phrase 'if true, would render... redundant' implies the claim is highly improbable and likely an overstatement.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"A new language app boasts 'fluency in a month,' a bold assertion that, if true, would render years of linguistic research and immersion programs largely redundant.","question":"What does the writer suggest about the app's promise?","options":["It is a revolutionary breakthrough.","It is likely an exaggeration.","It validates traditional methods.","It targets advanced learners."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.20","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The 'conspicuous omission' of a potentially relevant factor suggests the report was not entirely objective, implying bias.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The news report on the economic downturn meticulously detailed various contributing factors, yet conspicuously omitted any mention of the recent tax cuts for corporations.","question":"What does the passage imply about the news report?","options":["It was comprehensive and balanced.","It subtly presented a biased perspective.","It focused solely on consumer behavior.","It advocated for further tax reforms."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.reading.21","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Read, then choose the best answer.","explanation":"The passage implies the park's main goal was to boost property values, suggesting an underlying economic motive over ecological one.","kind":"multiple_choice","passage":"The city's new 'eco-park' was unveiled with much fanfare, though its primary function appeared to be increasing property values in the adjacent luxury apartment complex, rather than ecological preservation.","question":"What does the writer imply about the 'eco-park'?","options":["It genuinely prioritizes ecology.","Its true purpose is economic gain.","It is a model for urban green spaces.","It was funded by public donations."],"answer":1},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.01","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Inversion with 'so' + adjective/adverb at the beginning of a sentence requires the auxiliary verb to precede the subject.","kind":"transformation","source":"Her dedication to the project was so complete that she often worked through the night.","instruction":"Begin with 'So...'.","answers":["So complete was her dedication to the project that she often worked through the night."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.02","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"'Lest' introduces a negative purpose clause, followed by a mandative subjunctive (the base form of the verb).","kind":"transformation","source":"The researchers took great care to document every step of the experiment because they wanted to avoid any potential misinterpretation of their findings.","instruction":"Use the word 'lest'.","answers":["The researchers took great care to document every step of the experiment lest their findings be misinterpreted.","The researchers took great care to document every step of the experiment lest there be any potential misinterpretation of their findings."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.04","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Inverted mixed conditionals omit 'if' and place the auxiliary 'had' before the subject in the past perfect clause.","kind":"transformation","source":"If you had followed the recipe exactly, the cake would be perfect now.","instruction":"Begin with 'Had...'.","answers":["Had you followed the recipe exactly, the cake would be perfect now."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.07","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"This transformation uses inversion after 'so' when it begins a sentence, placing the auxiliary verb before the subject for emphasis.","kind":"transformation","source":"The therapist's advice was so profound that it completely changed my perspective on stress management.","instruction":"Begin with 'So profound'.","answers":["So profound was the therapist's advice that it completely changed my perspective on stress management."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.08","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"The conjunction 'lest' introduces a negative purpose clause, requiring the mandative subjunctive form of the verb (base form without 'to').","kind":"transformation","source":"The professor emphasized the importance of citing sources correctly to avoid accusations of plagiarism.","instruction":"Use the word 'lest'.","answers":["The professor emphasized the importance of citing sources correctly lest students be accused of plagiarism.","The professor emphasized the importance of citing sources correctly lest they be accused of plagiarism.","The professor emphasized the importance of citing sources correctly lest there be accusations of plagiarism."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.12","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"This uses an inverted conditional structure ('Had it not been for') to express a past condition with a present result.","kind":"transformation","source":"If my parents hadn't encouraged my creativity when I was young, I wouldn't be an artist today.","instruction":"Begin with 'Had it not been for'.","answers":["Had it not been for my parents' encouragement of my creativity when I was young, I wouldn't be an artist today."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.13","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Inversion with \"so + adjective\" at the beginning of a sentence requires the auxiliary verb to precede the subject.","kind":"transformation","source":"The company's financial struggles were so severe that it had to declare bankruptcy.","instruction":"Begin with 'So severe'.","answers":["So severe were the company's financial struggles that it had to declare bankruptcy."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.14","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"\"Lest\" introduces a negative purpose clause, followed by the base form of the verb (mandative subjunctive) or \"should + base form\".","kind":"transformation","source":"The lecturer spoke slowly because she wanted to make sure no one missed any important details.","instruction":"Use the word 'lest'.","answers":["The lecturer spoke slowly lest anyone miss any important details."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.15","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"An absolute construction uses a noun phrase followed by a participle to add supplementary information, often indicating cause or circumstance.","kind":"transformation","source":"The train was delayed for hours, so many passengers missed their connecting flights.","instruction":"Begin with 'The train'.","answers":["The train having been delayed for hours, many passengers missed their connecting flights.","The train, delayed for hours, caused many passengers to miss their connecting flights."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.17","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"Dense nominal style involves converting verbs and adjectives into nouns to create more concise and formal noun phrases.","kind":"transformation","source":"The way that languages are acquired by children is a complex process that researchers are still trying to fully understand.","instruction":"Begin with 'The process'.","answers":["The process of child language acquisition is complex and not yet fully understood by researchers.","The process by which children acquire languages is a complex one that researchers are still trying to fully understand."]},{"id":"ex.placement.c2.transform.18","level":"C2","grammarPointIds":[],"prompt":"Rewrite the sentence so it means the same.","explanation":"\"Alleged\" implies a claim without proof, while \"evaded\" precisely describes avoiding a question, adding nuance to the report.","kind":"transformation","source":"The journalist said that the politician had clearly avoided answering the direct question about the scandal.","instruction":"Use the word 'evaded'.","answers":["The journalist alleged that the politician had clearly evaded the direct question about the scandal.","The journalist said that the politician had clearly evaded the direct question about the scandal."]}]}